{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b4d284de3ed5b96a", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tAlignment Drift in Single-Model Speculative Decoding for ASR:\n2\tMechanism, Correction, and Cost\n3\tXinyu Wang1,2∗ , Huapeng Zhou1 , Ziyu Zhao2 , Silin Meng1 ,\n4\tKe Bai1 , Dongming Shen1 , Xiao-Wen Chang2 , Alex Smola1\n5\t1\n6\t\n7\t2\n8\t\n9\tBoson AI\n10\tMcGill University\n11\t\n12\tarXiv:2608.12703v1 [cs.SD] 13 Aug 2026\n13\t\n14\tAbstract\n15\tSpeculative decoding speeds up generation by letting a cheap\n16\tdraft propose several tokens that a target model checks in\n17\tone pass. In the single-model form, the draft is a lightweight\n18\tmodule attached to the target rather than a separate model.\n19\tApplying this design to Automatic Speech Recognition (ASR)\n20\tintroduces an extra problem. The draft can read the whole\n21\taudio at every step, yet its proposals get worse as it runs on its\n22\town. Access is not localization. The accepted text keeps the\n23\ttranscript position explicit, but the draft must also track the\n24\tchanging audio position. In the primary matched comparison,\n25\tper-step audio access changes the first proposal modestly\n26\tbut roughly doubles later-proposal acceptance. Fixed-width\n27\twindows show that the audio position explains part of this\n28\tgap. A correctly placed window recovers continuation, while\n29\tan equally narrow window at the wrong position reduces\n30\tit. Late-draft median error reaches 21 frames in the hardest\n31\treported condition, while target attention during verification\n32\tstays within a 2-frame median. We test two ways to reduce\n33\tthis drift. The first reads the audio position from verification\n34\tattention and uses it to guide the next draft round. It saves\n35\ttime only when the extra accepted tokens offset the readout\n36\tcost. The second is AnchorDraft, which teaches the draft\n37\tto track the audio position during training without changing\n38\tthe inference graph. The trained draft improves end-to-end\n39\tspeed at both tested target scales. These results show that ASR\n40\tself-speculation depends on token prediction, audio-position\n41\ttracking, and draft cost.\n42\t\n43\t1\n44\t\n45\tIntroduction\n46\t\n47\tLarge Language Models (LLMs) use AutoRegressive (AR)\n48\tdecoding, which requires one target-model pass per generated\n49\ttoken. Speculative decoding reduces the number of target\n50\tpasses while returning the target’s greedy output under exact\n51\tarithmetic. A cheaper draft proposes several tokens, and\n52\tthe target checks them in one forward pass and accepts the\n53\tlongest matching prefix (Leviathan, Kalman, and Matias\n54\t2023; Chen et al. 2023; Xia et al. 2023). Text systems obtain\n55\tthese proposals from a smaller model, auxiliary prediction\n56\theads, or early target layers (Cai et al. 2024; Li et al. 2025b,a;\n57\tZhang et al. 2024; Elhoushi et al. 2024). Their speed depends\n58\ton both the accepted draft length and the cost of producing\n59\tit (Yan, Agarwal, and Venkataraman 2025).\n60\t∗\n61\t\n62\tEmail: xinyu@boson.ai\n63\t\n64\tExtending this design to Automatic Speech Recognition\n65\t(ASR) adds a moving audio input to this problem. Paired\n66\tsystems use a second recognizer that follows the audio independently (Gandhi, von Platen, and Rush 2023; Wei et al.\n67\t2025), but require serving another recognizer. We instead\n68\tattach a lightweight draft to the target. It reuses the tokenizer,\n69\tencoded audio, and decoder cache while the target is idle.\n70\tThe draft must follow the audio and cost less than the target\n71\tpasses it replaces.\n72\tTo locate where proposals fail, we split each speculative\n73\tround. The restart is the first proposal after verification. Later\n74\tdraft-only proposals are continuation proposals. Per-step audio access changes restart acceptance modestly in our primary\n75\tmatched comparison but roughly doubles continuation acceptance. The main loss develops while the draft runs without\n76\tthe target.\n77\tDraft cost is a separate constraint. Drafts with per-step audio\n78\taccess reach 1.4–1.7× end-to-end speedup with WER close\n79\tto autoregressive decoding. A same-family full recognizer\n80\tproduces almost perfect proposals but is slower than autoregressive decoding. Useful proposals must reduce enough\n81\ttarget work to cover their cost.\n82\tThe restart and continuation split points to information that\n83\tchanges between target passes. The accepted prefix records\n84\twhich tokens have been generated. Audio adds a second\n85\trequirement. The draft reads the entire encoded audio at\n86\tevery step, yet it must still find the frame needed for its next\n87\ttoken. We call this frame the audio anchor. The text position\n88\tadvances one token at a time, while the audio anchor moves\n89\tby a variable number of frames because token duration varies.\n90\tLocation-aware and monotonic attention address a related\n91\tmoving-position problem in conventional speech decoding\n92\t(Chorowski et al. 2015; Chiu and Raffel 2018). Here, the\n93\tposition must be maintained by a shallow draft between\n94\ttarget passes. We use alignment drift for the growing distance\n95\tbetween the draft’s attention and the audio anchor during later\n96\tdraft steps.\n97\tContinuation decay could also reflect weak token features,\n98\tconfidence errors, or limited draft capacity. We isolate audio\n99\tposition by fixing the visible window width and changing\n100\tonly its center. A correct-position window recovers part of the\n101\tlost continuation, while a wrong-position window reduces it.\n102\tThe official-split replication gives the same ordering. Audio\n103\tposition explains part of the gap, but not every rejection.\n104\t\n105\t\fInternal probes separate this position from next-token information. In the hardest reported condition, late-draft median\n106\terror reaches 21 frames, compared with a 2-frame median\n107\tduring target verification.\n108\tThese measurements connect continuation loss to a position that verification still estimates accurately. We test two\n109\tcorrections. The runtime correction uses verification attention\n110\tto position the next draft round. AnchorDraft adds position\n111\tsupervision during training without changing inference. Both\n112\timprove continuation on the tested Qwen checkpoints. Their\n113\tend-to-end benefit depends on whether the accepted work\n114\tthey recover covers their cost, so all latency results use the\n115\treal cached loop.\n116\t\n117\t2\n118\t\n119\tRelated Work\n120\t\n121\tText Speculative Decoding. Lossless speculative decoding\n122\tverifies several tokens from a cheaper draft and returns the\n123\ttarget’s greedy sequence under exact arithmetic (Leviathan,\n124\tKalman, and Matias 2023; Chen et al. 2023). The same\n125\tdraft-and-verify pattern also applies to sequence-to-sequence\n126\tgeneration (Xia et al. 2023). Single-model methods either\n127\tattach proposal heads (Cai et al. 2024; Li et al. 2025b,a) or\n128\tdraft from early target layers (Zhang et al. 2024; Elhoushi et al.\n129\t2024). These designs avoid serving a separate draft model,\n130\tbut their speed still depends on the work accepted per round\n131\tand the cost of drafting (Yan, Agarwal, and Venkataraman\n132\t2025). MARS instead changes verification by relaxing strict\n133\ttoken matching when the target margin is small (Song et al.\n134\t2026). We keep greedy verification fixed and study the draft\n135\tstate between target passes.\n136\tSpeculative ASR. ASR systems draw proposals from a\n137\tsecond recognizer (Wei et al. 2025), an encoder-attached\n138\tlightweight decoder (Lim et al. 2025), connectionist temporal classification outputs (Saon et al. 2026), or transcript\n139\tmaps (Ho et al. 2025). Distil-Whisper provides a smaller\n140\tpaired recognizer, while Whisper-Medusa attaches parallel\n141\tprediction heads to the recognizer (Gandhi, von Platen, and\n142\tRush 2023; Segal-Feldman et al. 2024). Partially autoregressive masking and anticipatory recognition change the search\n143\tor input contract (Okabe and Yamamoto 2025; Yusuf et al.\n144\t2024). WhisperKit evaluates ReDrafter but removes it from\n145\tone final configuration when drafting overhead outweighs its\n146\tbenefit (Orhon et al. 2025; Cheng et al. 2024). Our setting\n147\tinstead uses a shallow draft attached to the target and asks\n148\twhy it loses proposals while reading fixed audio memory.\n149\tSpeech Alignment and Probes. Attention-based speech\n150\trecognizers learn an input-output alignment (Chan et al.\n151\t2016). Location-aware attention, monotonic attention, and\n152\tcoverage objectives constrain that alignment (Chorowski et al.\n153\t2015; Raffel et al. 2017; Chiu and Raffel 2018; Tachibana,\n154\tUenoyama, and Aihara 2018; See, Liu, and Manning 2017).\n155\tAnchorDraft applies this idea only to the shallow draft’s\n156\taudio attention. The frozen target remains unchanged, and\n157\tthe outcome is speculative acceptance and latency rather than\n158\talignment quality itself. Linear probes and logit-lens analysis\n159\tcan locate readable information inside the decoder, but probe\n160\taccuracy alone does not establish causal use (Tenney, Das,\n161\t\n162\tand Pavlick 2019; Hewitt and Liang 2019; nostalgebraist\n163\t2020). The Supplementary Material (Supp.) gives broader\n164\tsystem comparisons and timing decomposition in Section F\n165\tand Sections E.4 and F.3.\n166\t\n167\t3\n168\t\n169\tPreliminaries and Evaluation Setup\n170\t\n171\tSpeculative Loop. In each round, the draft proposes up to\n172\tK tokens, where K is the maximum draft length. The frozen\n173\ttarget checks the block in one pass and accepts the longest\n174\tprefix that matches its greedy output. Under exact arithmetic,\n175\tthis verification rule returns the same sequence as targetgreedy decoding. The first proposal after verification is the\n176\trestart. Proposals before the next verification are continuation\n177\tproposals. Let sk be the probability that the first k proposals\n178\tare accepted, with s0 =1. Conditional acceptance at depth\n179\tk is ack = sk /sk−1 . We report a1 as restart acceptance\n180\tand ac2 as continuation acceptance. The mean number of\n181\tPK\n182\taccepted draft tokens is L = k=1 sk . End-to-end speedup\n183\tis autoregressive greedy wall-clock divided by speculative\n184\twall-clock on the same utterances. Word Error Rate (WER)\n185\tmeasures recognition error. Anchor error is the absolute\n186\tdistance between an attention peak and its reference audio\n187\tframe. It is reported on the post-stride audio grid or in\n188\tmilliseconds. One grid frame is approximately 80 ms for the\n189\tQwen and Voxtral analyses in this paper. Acceptance and\n190\taccepted-length differences are absolute unless marked as\n191\trelative. We use speed gain for the relative percentage change\n192\tfrom a matched control. WER is reported in percent, and\n193\tWER changes are in percentage points. Qwen aggregates\n194\tweight the five evaluation sets equally within each seed and\n195\tthen weight seeds equally.\n196\tNumerical Output Agreement. The verification rule is\n197\tlossless under exact arithmetic, but the measured lowprecision loop is not always token-for-token identical to\n198\ta separately computed target trace. Across 150 utterances\n199\tper set, token agreement is 0.9705–0.9966, utterance agreement is 0.9267–0.9933, and WER differences are small and\n200\tsign-mixed. Full-precision recomputation attributes the differences to low-margin decisions. Supp. tables 4 and 5 gives\n201\tthe complete check.\n202\tModels and Protocol. The draft follows the direct-tokenprediction and multi-layer feature design of the Extrapolation\n203\tAlgorithm for Greater Language-model Efficiency (EAGLE3) (Li et al. 2025a). We add cross-attention from each draft\n204\tstep to the frozen audio encoder. We train drafts for two\n205\tQwen3-ASR scales and Voxtral-Mini, with Whisper as a\n206\tcross-architecture comparison (Shi et al. 2026; Liu et al.\n207\t2025; Radford et al. 2023). Evaluation uses LibriSpeech\n208\tclean and other, TED-LIUM, GigaSpeech, and FLEURS\n209\t(Panayotov et al. 2015; Hernandez et al. 2018; Chen et al.\n210\t2021; Conneau et al. 2022). The matched feasibility drafts\n211\tuse LibriSpeech train-clean-100. The main causal results use\n212\tofficial training data, development-only selection, and official\n213\ttest sets. Supp. Section A and Section C.1 give the partitions,\n214\tsample sizes, and matched configurations. Earlier checkpoints\n215\ttrained on prefixes from test-partition slices are retained only\n216\tas diagnostics in Supp. Section C. The Qwen experiments\n217\t\n218\t\f(a)\n219\t\n220\twith audio\n221\t\n222\t(b)\n223\tno audio\n224\t\n225\t0.75\n226\t\n227\tverify\n228\t\n229\tk =1\n230\t\n231\tk =2\n232\t\n233\tacceptance\n234\t\n235\tcorrect anchor\n236\t\n237\t0.50\n238\t\n239\tk =3\n240\t\n241\tverify\n242\t\n243\t2 frames\n244\t\n245\tdraft attention\n246\t\n247\tabout 2×\n248\t\n249\t0.25\n250\t\n251\tdrift\n252\t\n253\t0.00\n254\trestart\n255\t\n256\tcontinuation\n257\t\n258\t(c)\n259\tverify\n260\t\n261\t···\n262\t\n263\tdraft\n264\t\n265\t···\n266\t\n267\t6–21 frames\n268\t\n269\treset\n270\t\n271\taccess 6= localization\n272\t\n273\tFigure 1: Overview of alignment drift. (a) Per-step audio access changes restart modestly and separates continuation. (b) Draft\n274\tposition drifts between target verifications and resets after verification. (c) Late-draft and verification anchor errors.\n275\t\n276\tused for the main timing results run on one NVIDIA A10080GB GPU at batch size one in bfloat16. Autoregressive and\n277\tspeculative timing use the same utterances and start from\n278\tthe waveform, including preprocessing, audio encoding, and\n279\tcached decoding. The separate system comparisons report\n280\ttheir own hardware and timing protocol.\n281\tReal Cached-Loop Measurement. Fixed-prefix scoring\n282\tomits errors that enter later draft inputs. We therefore compare\n283\toffline scoring, chained survival on fixed prefixes, and the\n284\treal cached loop under one decoding setup. Mean accepted\n285\tlength falls from 9.4 under offline scoring to 4.83 under\n286\tchained survival and 4.35 in the real loop. Restart acceptance\n287\tfalls from 0.92 offline to 0.635 in the loop. Unless explicitly\n288\tlabeled as offline or chained diagnostics, reported acceptance\n289\tand latency results use the real cached loop. Supp. Section A\n290\tand Section F.2 give the decoding parameters and the full\n291\tcomparison of measurement protocols.\n292\tUncertainty. Unless stated otherwise, reported 95% confidence intervals (CIs) use paired utterance bootstrap resampling, with matched runs kept in each draw. They condition\n293\ton the trained checkpoints, so we report seed values or ranges\n294\tseparately. Depth and state analyses use 5,000 draws, while\n295\tcorrection timing uses 10,000 and treats timing repetitions\n296\tas blocks. The artifact includes the mapping rules, bootstrap\n297\tcode, figure data, and run metadata.\n298\tThe experiments first locate failure within the rollout, then\n299\tchange only the audio position, and finally test whether the\n300\trecovered work covers the cost of correction.\n301\t\n302\t4\n303\t\n304\tAlignment Drift\n305\t\n306\tWe first locate the acceptance loss within each speculative\n307\tround and show that audio-position error grows over the same\n308\tsteps. We then validate the anchor measure against an independent forced aligner and relate its error to proposal outcomes\n309\tacross draft depth. Finally, we change only the position of\n310\ta fixed-width audio window to test whether position affects\n311\tcontinuation.\n312\tRestart and Continuation. Per-step audio access can affect\n313\teither the first proposal after verification or the draft’s ability\n314\tto sustain later steps. These cases produce different acceptance\n315\t\n316\tAudio\n317\t\n318\ta1 ↑\n319\t\n320\tContinuation\n321\tac2 ↑\n322\t\n323\tSpeedup ↑\n324\t\n325\tQwen 1.7B\n326\t\n327\t✗\n328\t✓\n329\t\n330\t0.60–0.73\n331\t0.62–0.76\n332\t\n333\t0.25–0.32\n334\t0.54–0.58\n335\t\n336\t1.14–1.30×\n337\t1.41–1.55×\n338\t\n339\tVoxtral 3B\n340\t\n341\t✗\n342\t✓\n343\t\n344\t0.47–0.62\n345\t0.57–0.69\n346\t\n347\t0.22–0.25\n348\t0.71–0.78\n349\t\n350\t0.95–1.08×\n351\t1.42–1.69×\n352\t\n353\tTarget\n354\t\n355\tTable 1: Restart acceptance, continuation acceptance, and\n356\tspeedup for matched drafts with and without per-step audio\n357\tcross-attention. Each pair shares the model, layer budget,\n358\tLibriSpeech train-clean-100 data, and decoding loop. Ranges\n359\tgive the minimum and maximum over five evaluation sets.\n360\t\n361\tprofiles. We compare matched draft variants with and without\n362\tper-step audio cross-attention. Both drafts use the same target\n363\tfeatures, layer budget, training setup, and decoding loop. Only\n364\tthe draft with audio access cross-attends to the frozen audio\n365\trepresentation at every draft step.\n366\tThe matched drafts differ little at restart but separate during\n367\tcontinuation on every evaluation set. The audio-conditioned\n368\tdrafts also remain faster than autoregressive decoding. The\n369\tuseful effect of audio access therefore develops between\n370\ttarget passes rather than at the first proposal alone. Supp.\n371\ttable 6 gives the five set-level aggregates. A separate Whisper\n372\texperiment follows the same ordering under an earlier timing\n373\tprotocol and is reported in Supp. Section F.2.\n374\tCost Boundary. A stronger draft tests whether complete\n375\tASR state can prevent continuation decay. A Qwen3-ASR0.6B recognizer drafts for the 1.7B target with a shared\n376\ttokenizer, but it runs its own encoder and decoder at every\n377\tstep. It maintains nearly perfect restart and continuation acceptance, yet reaches only 0.59–0.70× autoregressive speed.\n378\tThis control separates position maintenance from cost. The\n379\trecognizer follows the audio, but its accepted tokens do not\n380\trepay its computation.\n381\tOfficial-Split Replication. To check whether the pattern\n382\tdepends on the feasibility checkpoint, we repeat the comparison on a speaker-stratified official split. Two independently\n383\tseeded drafts with per-step audio and one matched no-audio\n384\t\n385\t\fcontrol use the same training and evaluation protocol. The\n386\tcontinuation difference remains larger than the restart difference on every evaluation set. Its magnitude is smaller than in\n387\tthe feasibility runs, but its direction is unchanged. This replication shows that the restart and continuation ordering does\n388\tnot depend on the feasibility checkpoint. Supp. Section C.1\n389\treports the matched results.\n390\tThe feasibility, official-split, and training-correction experiments use different training sets and checkpoints. We compare\n391\tdirections across these experiments but do not treat their speed\n392\tvalues as repeated measurements of one configuration.\n393\tDrift over Draft Depth. The restart comparison locates the\n394\tloss between target passes but does not show how it develops.\n395\tWe therefore follow four draft steps on two official-split\n396\tseeds and all five evaluation sets. Anchor error rises while\n397\tsurvival falls, with the same slope directions in every seed\n398\tand set. Cumulative survival sk tracks the fraction of initial\n399\trounds that accept through depth k. Conditional acceptance\n400\tack shows where the curves separate, but at later depths it\n401\tcompares a different survivor population in each condition.\n402\tThe position intervention below reports both survival and\n403\tconditional acceptance.\n404\tValidating the Anchor Measure. Our analysis treats the\n405\tpeak of the draft’s audio cross-attention as its current audio position. We compare this measure with token frames\n406\tfrom the independent Massively Multilingual Speech Forced\n407\tAligner (MMS-FA) (Pratap et al. 2024). The two positions\n408\tare correlated both across and within utterances on every\n409\tevaluated set (fig. 2b). The higher Voxtral correlations do not\n410\timply lower absolute error. Its attention peak is systematically late relative to MMS-FA. Removing the set-level offset\n411\tsubstantially reduces the error. We therefore treat Voxtral as\n412\ta stable but offset boundary case, not as an example of exact\n413\tlocalization. The within-utterance agreement rules out utterance duration as the only explanation, so we use the attention\n414\tpeak as an operational position measure. A paired probe at\n415\tthe same restart events reads more position information after\n416\tverification than from the free-running draft state. The layer\n417\tand ridge strength are fixed on separate development data.\n418\tThe probe shows where position is readable but does not\n419\testablish causal use. Supp. tables 7 and 9 gives the anchor\n420\tvalidation and paired probe protocol.\n421\tAnchor Error and Proposal Outcome. Agreement with\n422\tthe independent aligner establishes that the attention peak\n423\ttracks audio position. We next compare accepted and rejected\n424\tproposals at each draft depth. Large errors are associated with\n425\trejection at restart, but the accepted and rejected distributions\n426\toverlap later in the rollout (fig. 2c). Anchor error is therefore\n427\trelated to proposal failure without explaining every rejection.\n428\tThe intervention below tests whether changing window position changes continuation acceptance. Supp. table 12 gives\n429\tthe depth-resolved values.\n430\tPosition Interventions. The prefix remains explicit during\n431\ta speculative round, while the relevant audio frame moves with\n432\tevery draft step. A correct-position window should recover\n433\tcontinuation if losing this position causes the observed decay.\n434\tAn equally restrictive wrong-position window provides the\n435\t\n436\tOutcome\n437\ts2\n438\ts3\n439\tL\n440\t\n441\tC−U\n442\t\n443\tW−U\n444\t\n445\tC−W\n446\t\n447\t+0.105\n448\t−0.122\n449\t+0.227\n450\t[+0.099, +0.113] [−0.129, −0.115] [+0.219, +0.236]\n451\t+0.083\n452\t−0.039\n453\t+0.122\n454\t[+0.077, +0.089] [−0.043, −0.035] [+0.116, +0.128]\n455\t+0.262\n456\t−0.370\n457\t+0.632\n458\t[+0.248, +0.276] [−0.386, −0.356] [+0.612, +0.653]\n459\t\n460\tTable 2: Official-split position-intervention effects over two\n461\tseeds and five evaluation sets. Entries are changes in sk\n462\tP4\n463\tand L =\n464\tk=1 sk with utterance-cluster bootstrap 95%\n465\tconfidence intervals. C, U, and W denote correct, unrestricted,\n466\tand the prespecified shifted window.\n467\t\n468\tmatched control. MMS-FA supplies a reference audio frame\n469\tfor each token, using the anchor measure validated in Section 4.\n470\tWe compare five attention conditions. Only the center of the\n471\tdraft’s cross-attention mask changes. Target verification, the\n472\taccepted prefix, draft weights, and window width remain\n473\tfixed. The unrestricted condition has no mask. The oracle\n474\twindow is centered on the current reference frame, while\n475\tthe pointer window extrapolates from previously verified\n476\tframes. The random condition samples an unrelated center.\n477\tThe shift condition applies a fixed offset to the reference center.\n478\tRandom and shift are therefore equally wide wrong-position\n479\tcontrols. Oracle tests the effect of a correct current position.\n480\tPointer tests whether positions from earlier verification steps\n481\tcan guide the next round. Both are offline interventions, not\n482\tdeployment sources. Supp. Sections A and C give the window\n483\tgeometry, width controls, and full intervention matrix.\n484\tThese conditions yield four comparisons. Wrong-position\n485\tversus unrestricted attention measures sensitivity to position. Correct-center versus unrestricted attention measures\n486\trecoverable acceptance. Correct-center versus an equally\n487\twide wrong-position window separates position from window\n488\twidth. The errors that remain under correct positioning show\n489\thow much of the failure has another source.\n490\tMatched Effect of Window Position. The official-split\n491\treplication provides the main causal check. All three arms\n492\tuse the same utterances, draft weights, and target verifier. At\n493\tdepth two, the correct-versus-shifted conditional-acceptance\n494\tcontrast is +0.254 with 95% CI [+0.241, +0.268] (fig. 2a).\n495\tThe complete depth curves show where this result holds.\n496\tCorrect positioning has a positive conditional-acceptance\n497\teffect through depth three. At depth four, the correct-versuswrong interval includes zero. Only 1.2% of unrestricted\n498\trounds reach that step, so the last estimate has little support.\n499\tSupp. table 12 reports survival, conditional acceptance, anchor\n500\terror, and matched contrasts at all four depths.\n501\tUnlike conditional acceptance, sk and L start from the\n502\tinitial round population. Correct centering raises both measures, while the shifted window lowers them (table 2). The\n503\tdirection is unchanged for either seed on every evaluation\n504\tset. Audio position therefore affects continuation. Survival\n505\tremains below one under correct centering, leaving a residual\n506\terror for other sources. Together with the independent anchor\n507\tvalidation and depth trend, this result supports alignment\n508\t\n509\t\f+0.254\n510\t0.2 +0.144\n511\t\n512\tcl\n513\tot\n514\ttd\n515\t\n516\t0.0\n517\t−0.2\n518\t\n519\t(b) Attention tracks position\n520\t\n521\tgg\n522\t-0.111\n523\tC−U\n524\t\n525\tW−U\n526\t\n527\tfl\n528\tC−W\n529\t\n530\t(c) Survival over draft depth\n531\tcumulative survival sk\n532\t\n533\tcontinuation change\n534\t\n535\t(a) Official position contrast\n536\t\n537\t0.0\n538\t0.4\n539\t0.8\n540\tcorrelation with aligner\n541\tshuffled\n542\t\n543\tattention\n544\t\n545\t0.6\n546\t0.4\n547\t0.2\n548\t0.0\n549\t\n550\t1\n551\t\n552\t2\n553\t3\n554\tdraft depth\n555\tcorrect\n556\tunrestricted\n557\t\n558\t4\n559\twrong\n560\t\n561\tFigure 2: Three measurements of audio position. (a) Depth-two conditional-acceptance contrasts on matched official-split\n562\texamples with two seeds and n=120 utterances per evaluation set. Error bars show paired 95% confidence intervals. C, U, and W\n563\tdenote correct, unrestricted, and shifted windows. (b) Correlation between the draft attention peak and an independent forced\n564\taligner, with shuffled controls. (c) Cumulative proposal survival under the three attention conditions.\n565\t\n566\tdrift as one source of continuation loss. Supp. table 12 gives\n567\tthe official matched comparison and the proposal-level decomposition. The correct-position gain persists over a broad\n568\tintermediate range of window widths. Narrow windows exclude the pointer’s own error, while wide windows re-admit\n569\tdistractors. Supp. Section A reports the sweep.\n570\tDiagnostic Boundaries. The causal result does not imply\n571\tthat every draft benefits from position correction. Two measurements determine its scope. Window sensitivity is the\n572\tacceptance gap between correct and wrong centers. Drift is\n573\tthe growth of anchor error under unrestricted attention.\n574\tThe official-split drafts establish position sensitivity\n575\tthrough depth three and replicate the restart-continuation\n576\tpattern. Earlier diagnostics show why window sensitivity and\n577\tdrift must remain separate. One 0.6B checkpoint responds\n578\tweakly to every window, while its official-split counterpart\n579\thas a correct-versus-wrong gap across two seeds. A sharedslice replay shows that the response depends strongly on the\n580\ttrained checkpoint. We test one possible cause by retraining\n581\tthe official draft with three levels of Gaussian noise added\n582\tto its training features. The correct-versus-wrong contrast\n583\tremains similar at later draft depths, so feature noise does not\n584\taccount for the checkpoint gap under this training recipe. Supp.\n585\tSection C.1, Section C, and table 14 gives these diagnostic\n586\tcomparisons.\n587\tThe Voxtral diagnostic supplies a second boundary. It\n588\tdistinguishes correct from wrong window centers, yet a\n589\trestrictive window can reduce acceptance even at the correct\n590\tposition. A useful correction therefore requires both position\n591\tsensitivity and loss of useful position information during freerunning continuation. These earlier diagnostics define the\n592\tscope of the official result rather than serving as its primary\n593\tevidence.\n594\t\n595\tPosition Information inside the Decoder. The window\n596\tintervention changes model input, but it does not show where\n597\tthe decoder carries position information. We therefore train\n598\theld-out linear probes on each decoder layer. The probe\n599\tpredicts the forced-alignment frame after removing the withinutterance trend with token rank. Anchor predictability peaks\n600\tin intermediate layers and weakens toward the output across\n601\tboth Qwen scales and Voxtral. The paired test in Section 4\n602\tcompares proposal-time and post-verification states at the\n603\tsame restart events. These probes show where audio position\n604\tis readable, not whether the decoder uses it causally (Hewitt\n605\tand Liang 2019). The matched window experiment tests\n606\tits effect on continuation. Supp. figs. 4b and 5 and table 9\n607\tgives the probe definitions, next-token curves, and held-out\n608\tintervals.\n609\t\n610\tAlternative Draft Changes. The final mechanism experiment tests simpler changes to the draft. Each alternative\n611\tchanges one property of the default draft and is measured\n612\tunder its own matched real-loop protocol. Later target layers\n613\tchange continuation by −0.037, a two-layer draft by ≈ 0, and\n614\tsoft KL training by −0.111. Their measured speed remains\n615\t0.99×–1.10×. Scheduled sampling improves continuation\n616\tby +0.054, while tree drafting increases accepted length\n617\tbut reduces speed from 1.60→1.56×. None improves the\n618\tquality-cost tradeoff. Broader official-split training improves\n619\tthe unrestricted draft, but a correct-position window still\n620\trecovers accepted length. Supp. Sections E.2 to E.3 report\n621\tthe matched protocols and full results.\n622\tThe forced-alignment pointer remains an offline intervention. Section 5 next tests a runtime correction that uses information available during decoding and a training correction\n623\tthat leaves the inference graph fixed.\n624\t\n625\t\f5\n626\t\n627\tCorrection and Cost\n628\t\n629\tTarget\n630\t\n631\tThe position interventions identify a recoverable part of the\n632\tcontinuation loss. A practical correction must recover enough\n633\taccepted work to offset its cost. We study two approaches.\n634\tThe runtime approach obtains a new position from each\n635\tverification pass. AnchorDraft instead teaches the draft to\n636\tmaintain its position during training.\n637\tCost Condition. Target verification accounts for 86–89%\n638\tof Qwen speculative wall-clock, while drafting accounts for\n639\t6.3–9.0%. A correction has room to help, but its gain must\n640\tcover its own cost. Runtime correction improves latency when\n641\t∆L\n642\t∆Ccorr\n643\t≳\n644\t,\n645\t1+L\n646\tKρ + 1\n647\t\n648\t(1)\n649\t\n650\twhere ∆L is the increase in accepted draft length, ∆Ccorr\n651\tis the correction cost relative to one target verification, and\n652\tρ is the cost of one draft step on the same scale. A matched\n653\tbatch-one Qwen 1.7B measurement gives ρ = 0.0275.\n654\tWe use this relation as a screening rule. Position must affect\n655\tprediction, the draft must lose useful position information\n656\tduring continuation, and the recovered accepted length must\n657\tcover the readout cost. Across the 19 settings in fig. 3c, the\n658\tmeasured result becomes positive only after the recovered\n659\twork clears this cost. The rule also matches the direction\n660\tof 10/10 deployed evaluation-set point estimates across the\n661\ttwo Qwen targets. This tests the sign, not the size, of the\n662\tspeed gain. Voxtral fails the second condition because its\n663\tposition error grows modestly while continuation remains\n664\thigh. Supp. Section E.4 and Sections C and E give the\n665\ttiming decomposition, acceptance results, and evaluation-set\n666\tintervals.\n667\tCandidate Runtime Signals. The intervention uses forced\n668\talignment, which is unavailable during normal decoding. We\n669\ttherefore ask whether the loop already contains a usable\n670\tposition estimate. We compare draft attention, speech-rate\n671\textrapolation, a delayed position from the previous round,\n672\tand verification attention. A usable source must be accurate\n673\tand current after each target pass.\n674\tA speech-rate prior reproduces only 17–32% of the correctwindow behavior. The previous-round position reproduces\n675\t64–66% but changes speed by −5.3–−0.8%. Verification\n676\tattention reproduces 83–86% and gives a +3.4% five-set\n677\tmean speed gain. It is the only tested source that is both\n678\tcurrent and cost-effective, although its clean interval includes\n679\tzero.\n680\tVerification-Attention Correction. The target’s audio attention during verification provides a fresh estimate on the\n681\ttested Qwen checkpoints. We average attention over heads at\n682\ta layer selected on development data and use its peak to center\n683\tthe next draft window. The implementation reads the existing\n684\tcache, uses no forced alignment, and adds no target forward\n685\tpass. The same rule selects layer 21 for two independently\n686\ttrained 1.7B drafts.\n687\tIn matched traces, verification reduces a late-draft error\n688\tof up to 21 frames to a 2-frame median, and the next restart\n689\tremains close to that position. After charging the readout\n690\tcost, the 1.7B correction improves the five-set mean. Its\n691\t\n692\t∆a1 /∆ac2 ↑\n693\t\n694\tSpeed [CI] ↑ Set range ↑\n695\t\n696\t0.6B +0.021/+0.111 +6.8% [+5.8%, +7.7%] 4.5–9.4%\n697\t1.7B +0.009/+0.050 +4.5% [+3.55%, +5.57%] 2.7–6.2%\n698\t\n699\tTable 3: AnchorDraft changes from matched controls over\n700\tfive equal-weighted evaluation sets. Set ranges contain the\n701\tper-set mean speed gains.\n702\tobserved seed-level gains span +3.0%–+3.8%, while the\n703\tclean interval includes zero. At 0.6B, the corresponding seed\n704\trange is +4.7%–+5.4%. Supp. table 16 and Section C.1\n705\treport the layer rule, readout cost, acceptance recovery, and\n706\tevaluation-set timing. The reset and timing result show that\n707\tthis position is current enough to guide the next rollout.\n708\tDeployment Scope. The runtime result above uses batchone, short-form requests. We vary batch size and audio\n709\tduration to test how far that result extends. Target verification\n710\tamortizes faster than drafting as batch size grows, so the\n711\tdraft-to-verification cost ratio rises from 0.0275 at batch one\n712\tto 0.1274 at batch 64. A separate real-loop sweep shows that\n713\tspeedup falls as audio duration grows from roughly 18 to\n714\t104 seconds. The reported gains therefore describe short,\n715\tbatch-one requests in the measured implementation. Supp.\n716\tSection E.4 gives both sweeps.\n717\tAnchorDraft Training Objective. Runtime correction\n718\treads and passes a new position after every verification.\n719\tAnchorDraft instead teaches the existing draft to maintain\n720\tthat position during training. It applies a Gaussian guidedattention target (Tachibana, Uenoyama, and Aihara 2018) to\n721\tthe shallow draft rather than the frozen target. Each training\n722\texample uses the same token sequence, target features, and\n723\tencoded audio as the control. Forced alignment maps the next\n724\ttoken at position t to frame ct . The first draft layer produces\n725\tattention At,hj from head h to audio\n726\tPindex j. We average\n727\tover heads to obtain Āt (j) = H −1 h At,hj and match it\n728\tto the normalized Gaussian qt (j) ∝ exp[−(j − ct )2 /(2σ 2 )].\n729\tUnaligned token positions and padded audio keys are masked.\n730\tThe objective is\n731\tL = Ltok + 0.5Lfeat + λLanchor ,\n732\t\n733\t(2)\n734\t\n735\twhere Lanchor is cross-entropy between Āt and qt , with σ > 0\n736\tand λ ≥ 0.\n737\tTraining Protocol. Optimization updates only the draft.\n738\tWe select λ=0.1 and σ=5 on a fixed holdout and train\n739\tboth scales on the same 3,933-utterance mixture of official\n740\ttraining-set data. The aligner and auxiliary loss are absent at\n741\tinference, so AnchorDraft has the same decoding graph as\n742\tits control. Target verification still accepts only target-greedy\n743\ttokens. Supp. Sections D.3 and E.1 give the timestamp pairing,\n744\tmasking rules, selected settings, and sensitivity sweeps.\n745\tTraining Results. This experiment tests whether position\n746\tsupervision improves the continuation steps identified in\n747\tSection 4. Controls match the data, optimization, seed, and\n748\tdecoding graph.\n749\tTable 3 shows that the gain is concentrated in continuation rather than restart. Speed improves on every measured\n750\t\n751\t\f6\n752\t4\n753\t2\n754\t\n755\t10\n756\t0\n757\t\n758\t0\n759\t−2\n760\t\n761\t−10\n762\ter\n763\t\n764\tan h ED iga RS\n765\tcle ot T G EU\n766\tFL\n767\tlow drift\n768\tmedium drift\n769\thigh drift\n770\t\n771\t20\n772\tcost-inclusive gain (%)\n773\t\n774\t20\n775\tchange\n776\t\n777\tcost-inclusive gain (%)\n778\t\n779\t8\n780\t\n781\t(b) recovery and cost\n782\t\n783\tn er D\n784\ta\n785\tclea oth TE Gig\n786\tacceptance recovery\n787\tcost-inclusive latency\n788\t\n789\t(c) runtime conditions\n790\t\n791\t10\n792\t\n793\t0\n794\t\n795\t−10\n796\t\n797\t(d) shared correction\n798\t0.200\n799\t\n800\tpays\n801\tcorrect-position gain\n802\t\n803\t(a) 1.7B deployed correction\n804\t\n805\t0.175\n806\t\n807\t0.155\n808\t\n809\t0.150\n810\t0.6B 30%\n811\t0.109\n812\t\n813\t0.125\n814\t0.100\n815\t\n816\t1.7B 24%\n817\t0.111\n818\t\n819\t0.075\n820\t0.00\n821\t0.25\n822\tcorrect-position gain L\n823\t\n824\tQwen-1.7B\n825\tQwen-0.6B\n826\t\n827\t0.084\n828\t\n829\tcon\n830\t\n831\ttrol\n832\t\n833\tft\n834\trDra\n835\t\n836\tho\n837\tAnc\n838\t\n839\tVoxtral-3B\n840\t\n841\tFigure 3: Runtime and training-correction measurements. (a) Runtime speed change for the 1.7B target on five evaluation sets. (b)\n842\tChange in accepted length and cost-inclusive latency under the correct-position intervention. (c) Correct-position accepted-length\n843\tgain and cost-inclusive pointer timing across 19 settings. (d) Remaining correct-position gain before and after AnchorDraft.\n844\t\n845\tevaluation set, while the maximum absolute WER change is\n846\t0.01 percentage point. The five-set speed gain spans +6.4%–\n847\t+7.1% across the two 0.6B seeds and +3.9%–+5.0% across\n848\tthe three 1.7B seeds. The post-hoc 0.6B sensitivity sweep\n849\tremains positive around the selected weight (table 18). The\n850\tVoxtral control changes by −0.011 where position error remains stable during continuation.\n851\tShared Mechanism. The overlap experiment distinguishes\n852\tbetter position maintenance from a general increase in draft\n853\tconfidence. We apply the same correct-position intervention\n854\tbefore and after training. The remaining gain falls at both\n855\tscales, and both reduction intervals exclude zero (fig. 3d). The\n856\tgain remains positive after training. Alignment supervision\n857\ttherefore removes part of the position-sensitive continuation\n858\terror without replacing the runtime correction.\n859\tCombining the Corrections. The positive correct-position\n860\tgain after training suggests that the runtime readout can\n861\tstill recover useful work. We test this in a separate fiveset experiment at 1.7B. The control, AnchorDraft, and\n862\tthe stacked system reach 1.472×, 1.531×, and 1.562× over\n863\tautoregressive decoding, respectively. Adding runtime correction after training gives a further +1.8% gain with 95% CI\n864\t[+0.9%, +2.7%], while the maximum absolute WER change\n865\tremains 0.01 percentage points. The corrections remain useful together despite addressing some of the same continuation\n866\terror. Supp. table 20 gives absolute speed intervals.\n867\tSystem Boundary. We next test whether a complete recognizer provides a better quality-cost tradeoff than the shallow\n868\tdraft. In the same-slice comparison, the full recognizer adds\n869\t600M parameters and 1.46 GiB of static weights. It accepts\n870\tmore tokens but reaches only 0.59–0.70× autoregressive\n871\t\n872\tspeed. The shallow draft has 76.2M trainable parameters and\n873\treaches 1.44–1.61× on the same slices. Peak allocated memory remains target-dominated in these serial batch-one runs,\n874\tso the matched comparison reports added static weight rather\n875\tthan a peak-memory ratio. The full recognizer maintains\n876\tposition at too much cost, while the shallow draft preserves\n877\tthe quality-cost balance. A separate Whisper comparison\n878\tgives the same audio-access ordering. Supp. Sections D.3,\n879\tE.2 and F.2 and Section F give the training checks, seed-level\n880\tresults, and system protocols.\n881\t\n882\t6\n883\t\n884\tConclusion and Limitations\n885\t\n886\tAlignment drift is one source of continuation failure in singlemodel speculative ASR. The restart and continuation split\n887\tlocates the loss between target passes. Correct-position interventions recover part of the loss, while wrong-position\n888\twindows reduce acceptance. Verification attention supplies\n889\ta fresh position during decoding. AnchorDraft trains the\n890\tdraft to maintain it without changing inference. Both improve\n891\tcontinuation, provided that the recovered accepted length\n892\tcovers the correction cost.\n893\tThe position intervention explains only part of the rejected\n894\tproposals. The study uses greedy verification that is lossless\n895\tunder exact arithmetic, while low-precision computation\n896\tcan change a tied argmax. AnchorDraft requires token-toframe supervision. Runtime correction is tested at two Qwen\n897\tscales under low-batch, short-form decoding with a separate\n898\treadout. Other architectures and serving conditions require\n899\tnew measurements. Extension to other continuous-memory\n900\ttasks remains a hypothesis.\n901\t\n902\t\fReferences\n903\tCai, T.; Li, Y.; Geng, Z.; Peng, H.; Lee, J. D.; Chen, D.; and\n904\tDao, T. 2024. MEDUSA: Simple LLM inference acceleration\n905\tframework with multiple decoding heads. In Proceedings\n906\tof the 41st International Conference on Machine Learning,\n907\tICML’24. JMLR.org.\n908\tChan, W.; Jaitly, N.; Le, Q.; and Vinyals, O. 2016. Listen, attend and spell: A neural network for large vocabulary\n909\tconversational speech recognition. In 2016 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing\n910\t(ICASSP), 4960–4964.\n911\tChen, C.; Borgeaud, S.; Irving, G.; Lespiau, J.-B.; Sifre, L.;\n912\tand Jumper, J. 2023. Accelerating Large Language Model\n913\tDecoding with Speculative Sampling. arXiv:2302.01318.\n914\tChen, G.; Chai, S.; Wang, G.; Du, J.; Zhang, W.-Q.; Weng,\n915\tC.; Su, D.; Povey, D.; Trmal, J.; Zhang, J.; Jin, M.; et al. 2021.\n916\tGigaSpeech: An Evolving, Multi-domain ASR Corpus with\n917\t10,000 Hours of Transcribed Audio. In Proc. Interspeech\n918\t2021.\n919\tCheng, Y.; Zhang, A.; Zhang, X.; Wang, C.; and Wang, Y.\n920\t2024. Recurrent Drafter for Fast Speculative Decoding in\n921\tLarge Language Models. arXiv:2403.09919.\n922\tChiu, C.-C.; and Raffel, C. 2018. Monotonic Chunkwise\n923\tAttention. In International Conference on Learning Represe