e Execution for Accelerating Seq2seq Generation. In Bouamor,\n1047\tH.; Pino, J.; and Bali, K., eds., Findings of the Association\n1048\tfor Computational Linguistics: EMNLP 2023, 3909–3925.\n1049\tSingapore: Association for Computational Linguistics.\n1050\tYan, M.; Agarwal, S.; and Venkataraman, S. 2025. Decoding\n1051\tSpeculative Decoding. In Proceedings of the 2025 Conference\n1052\t\n1053\tof the Nations of the Americas Chapter of the Association for\n1054\tComputational Linguistics: Human Language Technologies,\n1055\t6460–6473. Association for Computational Linguistics.\n1056\tYusuf, B.; Baskar, M. K.; Rosenberg, A.; and Ramabhadran, B. 2024. Speculative Speech Recognition by\n1057\tAudio-Prefixed Low-Rank Adaptation of Language Models.\n1058\tarXiv:2407.04641.\n1059\tZhang, J.; Wang, J.; Li, H.; Shou, L.; Chen, K.; Chen, G.;\n1060\tand Mehrotra, S. 2024. Draft& Verify: Lossless Large Language Model Acceleration via Self-Speculative Decoding.\n1061\tIn Proceedings of the 62nd Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (Volume 1: Long Papers),\n1062\t11263–11282. Association for Computational Linguistics.\n1063\t\n1064\t\fSupplementary Material\n1065\tAlignment Drift in Single-Model Speculative Decoding\n1066\tfor ASR:\n1067\tMechanism, Correction, and Cost\n1068\tThis supplement provides the experimental protocol and supporting evidence for the main paper. It reports the shared\n1069\tmeasurement and training settings, internal analyses, matched\n1070\tposition interventions, correction results, deployment conditions, and cross-architecture comparisons.\n1071\t\n1072\tA\n1073\t\n1074\tExperimental Protocol and Exactness\n1075\t\n1076\tThis section specifies the evaluation scope, measurements,\n1077\tcached decoding, interventions, implementation details, and\n1078\tnumerical exactness check used throughout the paper.\n1079\t\n1080\tA.1\n1081\t\n1082\tData and Measurement\n1083\t\n1084\tEvaluation scope. The experiments use drafts for two\n1085\tQwen3-ASR target scales and Voxtral-Mini. Whisper provides\n1086\ta cross-architecture comparison (Shi et al. 2026; Liu et al.\n1087\t2025; Radford et al. 2023). Evaluation uses LibriSpeech\n1088\tclean and other, TED-LIUM, GigaSpeech, and FLEURS\n1089\t(Panayotov et al. 2015; Hernandez et al. 2018; Chen et al. 2021;\n1090\tConneau et al. 2022). The feasibility drafts are trained on about\n1091\t6,000 utterances from LibriSpeech train-clean-100. They are\n1092\tevaluated on the five sets listed above. The official-split and\n1093\tAnchorDraft training protocols appear in Sections C.1\n1094\tand D.3. Those experiments use official test sets and provide\n1095\tthe causal and correction estimates. The checkpoint and\n1096\tarchitecture analyses in table 13 use separate models trained\n1097\ton prefixes from test-partition slices.\n1098\tAcceptance measures. Let K be the maximum draft length.\n1099\tWe write a1 for acceptance of the first proposal after verification and ack for acceptance at depth k among rounds\n1100\tthat reach that depth. Cumulative survival sk is the fraction\n1101\tof initial rounds whose first k proposals are accepted, and\n1102\tPK\n1103\tL = k=1 sk is the mean number of accepted draft tokens\n1104\tper round. Conditional acceptance describes the rounds that\n1105\tsurvive to a given depth; sk and L measure the cumulative\n1106\teffect over all initial rounds.\n1107\tPosition, speed, and error measures. Anchor error is the\n1108\tabsolute distance between an attention peak and its reference\n1109\taudio frame. Frame errors use the post-stride audio grid, where\n1110\tone frame is approximately 80 ms for the Qwen and Voxtral\n1111\tanalyses. Cross-architecture validation in tables 7 and 10 uses\n1112\tmilliseconds, while depth-resolved and runtime analyses use\n1113\tframes. Signed offsets are labeled explicitly. Acceptance and\n1114\taccepted-length differences are absolute unless marked as\n1115\trelative. Speed gain is the relative percentage change from\n1116\ta matched control. WER is reported in percent, and WER\n1117\tchanges are in percentage points. For Qwen, we average the\n1118\tfive evaluation sets equally within each seed and then average\n1119\tthe seeds equally.\n1120\tTiming protocol. The Qwen correction measurements reported in the main paper use one NVIDIA A100-80GB GPU\n1121\t\n1122\tat batch size one in bfloat16. Timing starts from the waveform and includes preprocessing, audio encoding, and cached\n1123\tdecoding. Autoregressive and speculative runs use the same\n1124\tutterances, warm-up, decode limits, and target cache. Each\n1125\treported speedup is autoregressive wall-clock divided by\n1126\tspeculative wall-clock. Separate harnesses and hardware are\n1127\tidentified in Section F.\n1128\tUncertainty. Unless stated otherwise, brackets give 95%\n1129\tconfidence intervals (CIs). For official-split acceptance contrasts, we resample utterances 5,000 times with random seed\n1130\t42 and apply the same sampled indices to every matched condition. These intervals measure variation across evaluation\n1131\tutterances for a fixed trained checkpoint. Seed-level values or\n1132\tranges separately describe training-seed variation. The paired\n1133\tstate probe and table 15 use the same 5,000-draw procedure.\n1134\tRuntime and AnchorDraft intervals use 10,000 paired utterance resamples with random seed 20260725. Runtime\n1135\ttiming uses 100 measured repetitions after 20 warm-up runs.\n1136\tControl and corrected measurements are paired by utterance\n1137\tand timing repetition.\n1138\t\n1139\tA.2\n1140\t\n1141\tDecoding and Intervention Protocol\n1142\t\n1143\tReal cached loop. Offline scoring evaluates each proposal\n1144\ton a fixed target prefix. Chained scoring propagates the\n1145\tresulting survival probabilities across depths, but it still omits\n1146\tthe draft states produced by earlier accepted proposals. The\n1147\treal cached loop feeds accepted tokens and their cached states\n1148\tinto the next draft step. Unless explicitly labeled as offline or\n1149\tchained diagnostics, reported acceptance and latency use the\n1150\treal cached loop.\n1151\tAttention conditions. The unrestricted arm can attend to\n1152\tthe full encoded audio. Correct-position, pointer, random,\n1153\tand shifted arms use the same fixed-width attention mask\n1154\tand differ only in its center. The correct center comes from\n1155\tforced alignment. The pointer extrapolates from previously\n1156\tverified frames. Random and shifted centers provide matched\n1157\twrong-position controls. The pointer is an offline intervention.\n1158\tThe deployed correction instead reads verification attention.\n1159\tWindow geometry. All restricted attention conditions use\n1160\ta ±400 ms half-width. The shifted center moves 500 ms\n1161\tforward and is clipped to the valid audio grid. The random\n1162\tcenter is sampled uniformly over that grid with a fixed seed of\n1163\t0. The ±400 ms half-width was fixed before test evaluation.\n1164\tWindow-width robustness. The width sweep tests whether\n1165\tthe position effect depends on the selected half-width. On\n1166\tTED-LIUM and GigaSpeech, we vary the half-width while\n1167\tholding the window center and decoder fixed. The correctposition gain persists over a broad intermediate range. Narrow\n1168\twindows exclude pointer error, while wide windows re-admit\n1169\tdistractors. The position effect therefore does not depend on\n1170\tthe single ±400 ms setting on these two evaluation sets.\n1171\t\n1172\tA.3\n1173\t\n1174\tTraining and Implementation\n1175\t\n1176\tDraft architecture. The Qwen draft combines four frozen\n1177\ttarget representations with the current token embedding. One\n1178\tcausal block then applies self-attention, cross-attention to\n1179\t\n1180\t\fthe frozen audio representation, and a feed-forward layer.\n1181\tThe 0.6B configuration uses hidden width 1024, 16 attention\n1182\theads, and feed-forward width 2048, for 19.2M trainable\n1183\tparameters. The 1.7B configuration uses hidden width 2048,\n1184\t16 heads, and feed-forward width 4096, for 76.2M trainable\n1185\tparameters. Audio cross-attention is evaluated at every draft\n1186\tstep.\n1187\tAlignment mapping. Each stored target sequence is decoded without special tokens. Its lowercase alphanumeric text\n1188\tis paired with the corresponding official training utterance\n1189\tby character-bigram overlap. A pair must score at least 0.45,\n1190\tand extraction stops if more than 5% of utterances remain unmatched. The Massively Multilingual Speech Forced Aligner\n1191\t(MMS-FA) supplies character times (Pratap et al. 2024). For\n1192\teach token, we decode its cumulative prefix and assign the\n1193\ttoken the time of its last newly added alphanumeric character. Repeated tokens use their newly decoded characters.\n1194\tPunctuation that adds no retained character reuses the latest\n1195\tvalid time. Silence is not assigned a standalone token target.\n1196\tInitial special positions and tokens without a valid character\n1197\talignment are excluded from the alignment loss. Times are\n1198\tconverted to the utterance-specific audio grid and checked\n1199\tagainst its expected frame rate.\n1200\tTraining. The 3,933-utterance official training-set mixture\n1201\tcontains 1,797 Clean, 598 Other, 598 TED-LIUM, 300 GigaSpeech, and 640 FLEURS utterances. Training runs for\n1202\t45 epochs with batch size 12, AdamW at learning rate 10−3 ,\n1203\tweight decay 0.01, cosine annealing, gradient clipping at\n1204\t1.0, and feature noise 0.6. The objective combines token\n1205\tcross-entropy, a smooth-L1 feature loss weighted by 0.5, and\n1206\tthe alignment loss in Eq. (6). A fixed holdout selects λ = 0.1\n1207\tand σ = 5, and the reported model uses the final training\n1208\tepoch. The Gaussian target is centered on the frame for the\n1209\tnext token, masked at padded frames, and renormalized over\n1210\tvalid audio positions.\n1211\tRuntime layer. We evaluate layers {7, 14, 18, 21, 24, 27}\n1212\ton development data. The same procedure selects layer 21 for\n1213\tall four evaluated Qwen heads, and this layer remains fixed\n1214\tfor every test set.\n1215\t\n1216\tA.4\n1217\t\n1218\tNumerical Exactness\n1219\t\n1220\tSpeculative verification reproduces target-greedy decoding\n1221\tunder exact arithmetic. To test whether finite-precision execution preserves this property, we decode 150 utterances\n1222\tper evaluation set with K=2, use bfloat16 verification, and\n1223\trecompute the logits in float32. The bfloat16 implementation\n1224\tis not sequence-exact. Float32 recomputation changes the\n1225\targmax in 0.14–0.31% of token positions, and the recorded\n1226\tbfloat16 margins at these positions are small.\n1227\tAggregate WER can hide degradation among the utterances\n1228\twhose output changes. We therefore analyze that group on\n1229\tGigaSpeech, the set with the lowest utterance agreement.\n1230\t\n1231\tQuantity\n1232\t\n1233\tValue\n1234\t\n1235\tMismatched utterances\n1236\t11/150\n1237\tMismatched token positions\n1238\t244/8,113\n1239\tMean WER difference\n1240\t−0.193\n1241\tPaired 95% interval\n1242\t[−0.893, +0.483]\n1243\tMedian WER difference\n1244\t0.000\n1245\tLower, equal, higher fractions 0.182/0.727/0.091\n1246\tPrecision argmax flip fraction\n1247\t0.0017\n1248\tFlip-position median margin\n1249\t0.00\n1250\t\n1251\tTable 5: Mismatch-group WER and full-sample precision\n1252\tchecks on GigaSpeech. WER differences are speculative\n1253\tminus autoregressive in percentage points.\n1254\tThe paired utterance bootstrap uses 5,000 draws. Its interval\n1255\tcrosses zero, and most mismatched utterances retain the\n1256\tsame WER. Positions where low- and full-precision argmax\n1257\tdiffer have zero median low-precision margin. We detect no\n1258\tsystematic WER increase in this sample.\n1259\tPrecision propagation bound. Let q be the probability that\n1260\tnumerical precision changes a verified argmax at any token,\n1261\tand let n be the output length. If these events are independent,\n1262\tthen\n1263\tPr(utterance exact) ≥ (1 − q)n ,\n1264\t(3)\n1265\tn+1\n1266\tE[token-mismatch fraction] ≤\n1267\tq.\n1268\t2\n1269\tThe first bound requires no changed argmax in the utterance.\n1270\tFor the second, a mismatch at position j requires a changed\n1271\targmax at or before j, whose probability is at most jq. Greedy\n1272\tdecoding can return to the same suffix, so both expressions\n1273\tare conservative. This explains why a small per-token argmax\n1274\tflip rate can reduce utterance-level exact match while leaving\n1275\tWER nearly unchanged.\n1276\t\n1277\tB\n1278\t\n1279\tEvidence for Alignment Drift\n1280\t\n1281\tPer-step audio access matters primarily during continuation,\n1282\tand alignment drift explains part of the resulting loss. This\n1283\tsection locates the audio-access effect within a speculative\n1284\tround, validates the attention-peak anchor, examines token\n1285\tand position information in model states, and relates anchor\n1286\terror to proposal outcome.\n1287\t\n1288\tB.1\n1289\t\n1290\tAudio Access and Continuation\n1291\t\n1292\tTo locate the effect of per-step audio access within a speculative round, we separate the first proposal from later proposals.\n1293\tMatched drafts share the target, training procedure, evaluation\n1294\texamples, and real cached loop. They differ only in whether\n1295\tthe draft uses audio cross-attention at each step.\n1296\tAudio access changes restart acceptance modestly on every\n1297\tset, while the continuation difference is much larger. The\n1298\tseparation localizes most of the benefit of audio access to the\n1299\tproposals generated between target passes.\n1300\t\n1301\tB.2\n1302\t\n1303\tValidating the Attention-Peak Anchor\n1304\t\n1305\tWe measure the audio anchor as the peak of audio crossattention. We compare its time with an independent MMS-FA\n1306\tforced-alignment frame (Pratap et al. 2024). Pooled correlation measures whether the two positions move together across\n1307\t\n1308\t\fSet\n1309\t\n1310\tToken exact ↑\n1311\t\n1312\tUtterance exact ↑\n1313\t\n1314\tAR WER (%) ↓\n1315\t\n1316\tSpeculative WER (%) ↓\n1317\t\n1318\tArgmax flip rate ↓\n1319\t\n1320\t0.9966\n1321\t0.9769\n1322\t0.9792\n1323\t0.9705\n1324\t0.9931\n1325\t\n1326\t0.9933\n1327\t0.9400\n1328\t0.9667\n1329\t0.9267\n1330\t0.9733\n1331\t\n1332\t3.52\n1333\t4.03\n1334\t4.63\n1335\t10.95\n1336\t4.36\n1337\t\n1338\t3.52\n1339\t4.15\n1340\t4.65\n1341\t10.93\n1342\t4.38\n1343\t\n1344\t0.0023\n1345\t0.0031\n1346\t0.0015\n1347\t0.0017\n1348\t0.0014\n1349\t\n1350\tClean\n1351\tOther\n1352\tTED-LIUM\n1353\tGigaSpeech\n1354\tFLEURS\n1355\t\n1356\tTable 4: Low-precision agreement with target-greedy decoding over 150 utterances per evaluation set. The argmax flip rate\n1357\tcompares low- and full-precision recomputation.\n1358\tSet\n1359\t\n1360\tClean\n1361\tOther\n1362\tTED-LIUM\n1363\tGigaSpeech\n1364\tFLEURS\n1365\t\n1366\tRestart acceptance ↑\n1367\t\n1368\tContinuation acceptance ↑\n1369\t\n1370\tWith audio\n1371\t\n1372\tWithout audio\n1373\t\n1374\tDifference\n1375\t\n1376\tWith audio\n1377\t\n1378\tWithout audio\n1379\t\n1380\tDifference\n1381\t\n1382\t0.757\n1383\t0.739\n1384\t0.746\n1385\t0.687\n1386\t0.616\n1387\t\n1388\t0.730\n1389\t0.730\n1390\t0.725\n1391\t0.661\n1392\t0.597\n1393\t\n1394\t0.027\n1395\t0.009\n1396\t0.021\n1397\t0.026\n1398\t0.019\n1399\t\n1400\t0.540\n1401\t0.551\n1402\t0.567\n1403\t0.543\n1404\t0.575\n1405\t\n1406\t0.249\n1407\t0.258\n1408\t0.303\n1409\t0.311\n1410\t0.320\n1411\t\n1412\t0.291\n1413\t0.293\n1414\t0.264\n1415\t0.232\n1416\t0.255\n1417\t\n1418\tTable 6: Restart and continuation acceptance for matched drafts with and without per-step audio access.\n1419\t\n1420\tutterances. Within-utterance correlation removes differences\n1421\tin utterance length, and absolute error measures their distance.\n1422\tShuffled targets set a reference floor. We use the peak as an\n1423\toperational position measure while allowing the decoder to\n1424\tencode position elsewhere.\n1425\tThe attention peak tracks the independent frame both across\n1426\tutterances and within each utterance. This agreement supports\n1427\tits use as a measure of audio position.\n1428\tVoxtral offset. Voxtral’s higher correlation does not mean\n1429\tthat its attention peak is closer to the forced-alignment frame.\n1430\tThe median signed offset is +205 ms on Clean and +165\n1431\tms on Other. Subtracting the set-level median offset reduces\n1432\tthe median absolute error from 230 to 139 ms on Clean and\n1433\tfrom 186 to 97 ms on Other. All 25 utterance-level offsets are\n1434\tpositive on each set. The peak therefore follows the movement\n1435\tof the speech position but remains systematically later than\n1436\tMMS-FA.\n1437\t\n1438\tB.3\n1439\t\n1440\tToken and Position Information in Model\n1441\tStates\n1442\t\n1443\tProbe protocol. The token probe asks when the next token\n1444\tbecomes predictable in the decoder. At each layer, it predicts\n1445\tthe final-layer feature and applies the frozen output projection.\n1446\tGPT-2 negative log-likelihood (NLL) divides tokens into\n1447\teasier and harder text-prediction groups (Radford et al. 2019).\n1448\tWe decode each model token with its prefix, retokenize the\n1449\tnew text with GPT-2, and average NLL over that text span.\n1450\tThese groups measure text predictability rather than linguistic\n1451\tor acoustic categories.\n1452\tThe anchor probe asks where audio position is readable. It\n1453\tpredicts the forced-alignment frame after removing the withinutterance trend with token rank. Both probes use separate\n1454\ttraining and evaluation utterances, and shuffled labels set the\n1455\tfloor. We summarize each layerwise curve by its center of\n1456\tmass (COM), which weights each layer index by its probe\n1457\tscore.\n1458\t\n1459\tToken information across layers. Across model families,\n1460\ttokens that are difficult to predict from text alone have a\n1461\tlater center-of-mass depth. The layerwise curves show the\n1462\tsame ordering. This experiment identifies when next-token\n1463\tinformation becomes readable. The audio-position probe\n1464\tbelow measures a separate quantity.\n1465\tPosition information across layers. The position probe\n1466\tmeasures where audio position can be decoded within the\n1467\tmodel. At each layer, a held-out linear model predicts the\n1468\tforced-alignment frame after the token-rank trend is removed.\n1469\tAnchor predictability peaks at layers 18 of 28, 17 of 28,\n1470\tand 13 of 30 for Qwen-1.7B, Qwen-0.6B, and Voxtral-3B. It\n1471\tthen falls by 64–70% at the output. This pattern differs from\n1472\tthe later rise of next-token information in fig. 4b. The two\n1473\tprobes therefore measure different information paths through\n1474\tthe decoder.\n1475\tDraft and post-verification states. Verification should\n1476\tleave more readable position information if it restores the\n1477\tdraft’s audio position. We compare draft and post-verification\n1478\tfeatures at the same restart event, using the same next-token\n1479\talignment target, utterance split, and bootstrap resample.\n1480\tLayer 20 and the ridge regularization strength are selected\n1481\ton a disjoint development slice and then held fixed. Both\n1482\tseeds use 600 utterances, and the held-out split contains 180\n1483\tutterances.\n1484\tPost-verification features contain more linearly readable\n1485\tposition information than draft features in both seeds.\n1486\t\n1487\tB.4\n1488\t\n1489\tAnchor Error and Proposal Outcome\n1490\t\n1491\tIf anchor error contributes to proposal failure, rejected proposals should have larger errors than accepted proposals.\n1492\tWe compare both outcomes at matched restart positions and\n1493\treport the odds of rejection above a 200 ms error threshold.\n1494\tRejected proposals have larger anchor error on every evaluation set, and the large-error rejection odds exceed one\n1495\t\n1496\t\fComparison\n1497\t\n1498\tSet or feature\n1499\t\n1500\tPooled r ↑\n1501\t\n1502\tUtterance r ↑\n1503\t\n1504\tError in ms ↓\n1505\t\n1506\tQwen-1.7B peak vs. MMS-FA\n1507\tQwen-1.7B peak vs. MMS-FA\n1508\tQwen-1.7B peak vs. MMS-FA\n1509\tQwen-1.7B peak vs. MMS-FA\n1510\tQwen-1.7B peak vs. MMS-FA\n1511\tVoxtral-3B peak vs. MMS-FA\n1512\tVoxtral-3B peak vs. MMS-FA\n1513\t\n1514\tClean\n1515\tOther\n1516\tTED-LIUM\n1517\tFLEURS\n1518\tGigaSpeech\n1519\tClean\n1520\tOther\n1521\t\n1522\t0.736\n1523\t0.754\n1524\t0.741\n1525\t0.780\n1526\t0.753\n1527\t0.928\n1528\t0.949\n1529\t\n1530\t0.728\n1531\t0.746\n1532\t0.750\n1533\t0.711\n1534\t0.756\n1535\t0.932\n1536\t0.961\n1537\t\n1538\t155\n1539\t149\n1540\t142\n1541\t136\n1542\t144\n1543\t230\n1544\t186\n1545\t\n1546\tTable 7: Attention-peak agreement with independent forced-alignment frames. Errors are measured in milliseconds.\n1547\tlinguistic\n1548\t\n1549\t1.0\n1550\t\n1551\tAco 0.6B\n1552\tAco 1.7B\n1553\t\n1554\t0.85\n1555\t\n1556\t0.75\n1557\t0.70\n1558\t0.65\n1559\t\n1560\t0.8\n1561\t0.6\n1562\t\n1563\tLing. COM 65%\n1564\t\n1565\t0.80\n1566\t\n1567\tAco Voxtral-3B\n1568\tLin 0.6B\n1569\t\n1570\t0.4\n1571\t0.2\n1572\t\n1573\t0.60\n1574\t\n1575\tLin 1.7B\n1576\tLin Voxtral-3B\n1577\t\n1578\tAcoustic COM 82%\n1579\t\n1580\tacoustic\n1581\t\n1582\tnext-token predictability\n1583\t\n1584\tCOM (fraction of depth)\n1585\t\n1586\t0.90\n1587\t\n1588\t0.0\n1589\t\n1590\tQwen Qwen Voxtral VoxtralWhisperWhisperWhisper\n1591\t0.6B 1.7B\n1592\t3B\n1593\t24B\n1594\tS\n1595\tM\n1596\tL\n1597\t\n1598\t0.0\n1599\t\n1600\t(a)\n1601\t\n1602\t0.2\n1603\t0.4\n1604\t0.6\n1605\t0.8\n1606\tlayer depth (fraction)\n1607\t\n1608\t1.0\n1609\t\n1610\t(b)\n1611\t\n1612\tFigure 4: Held-out next-token probes. (a) Center-of-mass decoder depth for low- and high-NLL tokens. (b) Next-token predictability\n1613\tby decoder layer.\n1614\t\n1615\tthroughout the table. These measurements link anchor error\n1616\tto proposal failure at restart.\n1617\tReconciling anchor-error summaries. The anchor-error\n1618\tsummaries condition on different events. Table 7 pools accepted and rejected restart proposals, whereas table 10 separates restart proposals by outcome. The pooled Qwen median\n1619\tof 136–155 ms lies between the accepted median of 42–48\n1620\tms and the rejected median of 208–369 ms. The frame-level\n1621\tvalues in the main paper describe later draft steps in the real\n1622\tcached loop. The summaries differ because they measure\n1623\tdifferent parts of the rollout.\n1624\tThe outcome comparison is associational. The official\n1625\tdepth-resolved analysis in table 12 measures the association\n1626\tover draft depth, and the matched window intervention in\n1627\tSection C supplies the causal test.\n1628\t\n1629\tC\n1630\t\n1631\tMatched Position Interventions\n1632\t\n1633\tPosition correction applies when position sensitivity and continuation drift co-occur. The official-split Qwen experiment\n1634\tbelow provides the primary causal estimate. The remaining\n1635\tcheckpoint and architecture comparisons define the boundary\n1636\tof that result.\n1637\t\n1638\tC.1\n1639\t\n1640\tOfficial-Split Position Effect\n1641\t\n1642\tOfficial-split protocol. The official-split replication samples 1,800 utterances from LibriSpeech train-clean-100 by\n1643\tfixed-seed speaker-stratified round robin and excludes utterances longer than 30 seconds. Training uses 45 epochs, batch\n1644\tsize 12, learning rate 10−3 , a one-layer draft, and feature noise\n1645\t0.6. The noise is Gaussian and scaled by the empirical standard\n1646\tdeviation of each input feature. Two audio-conditioned seeds\n1647\tand one matched no-audio draft are selected on dev-clean\n1648\tbefore evaluation on official test slices.\n1649\tRestart and continuation replication. Before interpreting\n1650\tthe position intervention, we verify the restart and continuation ordering under matched official training and evaluation\n1651\tpartitions. Two audio-conditioned seeds and one no-audio\n1652\tcontrol use the same target, training recipe, and real cached\n1653\tloop.\n1654\tThe audio-conditioned drafts have the larger advantage\n1655\tduring continuation on every evaluation set. This replication\n1656\tplaces the restart and continuation separation on the protocol\n1657\tused for the causal estimate.\n1658\tMatched position intervention. The primary causal contrast compares correct- and wrong-position windows with\n1659\tthe same width. The unrestricted reference has no mask and\n1660\tis not width-matched. All three conditions begin from the\n1661\t\n1662\t\fModel family\n1663\t\n1664\tHigh-NLL COM fraction\n1665\t\n1666\tHigh-low COM gap (layers)\n1667\t\n1668\t0.820\n1669\t0.811\n1670\t0.809\n1671\t0.803\n1672\t0.855\n1673\t0.806\n1674\t0.814\n1675\t\n1676\t4.6\n1677\t4.6\n1678\t5.3\n1679\t6.6\n1680\t2.2\n1681\t4.1\n1682\t5.5\n1683\t\n1684\tQwen-0.6B\n1685\tQwen-1.7B\n1686\tVoxtral-3B\n1687\tVoxtral-24B\n1688\tWhisper-small\n1689\tWhisper-medium\n1690\tWhisper-large\n1691\t\n1692\tTable 8: Probe summary averaged across five evaluation sets. COM fraction is the center-of-mass layer divided by decoder depth.\n1693\tThe gap is measured in decoder layers.\n1694\tDraft R2 ↑\n1695\t\n1696\tDraft seed\n1697\tbSTD\n1698\tbSTD-s1\n1699\tPooled\n1700\t\n1701\tPost-verification R2 ↑\n1702\t\n1703\tPaired difference ↑\n1704\t\n1705\t0.0365 [0.0290, 0.0460] 0.1956 [0.1638, 0.2328] +0.1591 [0.1319, 0.1899]\n1706\t0.0359 [0.0291, 0.0446] 0.1937 [0.1629, 0.2310] +0.1577 [0.1310, 0.1892]\n1707\t–\n1708\t– +0.1584 [0.1316, 0.1892]\n1709\t\n1710\tTable 9: Held-out position-probe results at matched restart events. Brackets give utterance-bootstrap 95% confidence intervals.\n1711\tThe pooled row reports only the paired difference.\n1712\t1.0\n1713\t0.8\n1714\tnet predictability\n1715\t\n1716\tC.2\n1717\t\n1718\tacoustic content (Qwen-1.