After correction, its cost and progress\n1927\tbecome 1 + Kρ + ∆Ccorr and 1 + L + ∆L. The corrected\n1928\tround is faster when\n1929\t1 + Kρ\n1930\t1 + Kρ + ∆Ccorr\n1931\t<\n1932\t.\n1933\t(4)\n1934\t1 + L + ∆L\n1935\t1+L\n1936\tCross-multiplication gives\n1937\t∆L\n1938\t∆Ccorr\n1939\t>\n1940\t.\n1941\t1+L\n1942\t1 + Kρ\n1943\t\n1944\t(5)\n1945\t\n1946\tThe main paper uses this approximation to predict the sign of\n1947\tthe speed change.\n1948\t\n1949\tD.2\n1950\tCross-architecture response. The final comparison asks\n1951\twhether sensitivity to window position and loss of position\n1952\tduring continuation occur together across model families.\n1953\tWithin each model, the five attention conditions use matched\n1954\tutterances and window widths.\n1955\tCorrect placement recovers acceptance for the drifting\n1956\tQwen draft. Voxtral distinguishes correct from wrong centers,\n1957\tbut restricting attention can reduce acceptance even at the\n1958\tcorrect center. Its attention peak remains stable across draft\n1959\tdepth despite its offset from MMS-FA. Position sensitivity\n1960\tand loss of position during continuation are therefore separate\n1961\tproperties.\n1962\t\n1963\tCorrection and Cost\n1964\t\n1965\tRuntime Correction\n1966\t\n1967\tRuntime anchor construction. The runtime source averages target audio attention over heads at a decoder layer\n1968\tselected on development data. We choose the earliest layer\n1969\twhose attention falls inside the reference window within\n1970\t0.02 of the best candidate. Both independently trained 1.7B\n1971\tdrafts select layer 21 from {7, 14, 18, 21, 24, 27}. Inference\n1972\trecomputes the selected attention scores from the existing key\n1973\tcache. It uses no forced alignment and no additional target\n1974\tforward pass.\n1975\tRequired anchor accuracy. The runtime source must be\n1976\taccurate enough for its accepted-length gain to cover its\n1977\tcost. We estimate this requirement using the pointer and\n1978