2091\tA parameter-free mixture predicts accepted length for noisy\n2092\tanchors. Across the tested noise levels, predicted and observed\n2093\taccepted length differ by at most 0.023.\n2094\t\n2095\t0.6B. At each scale, four of five set-level intervals are positive.\n2096\tThe measured cost rule matches all 10/10 evaluation-set\n2097\tand scale point-estimate signs. Correction pays where the\n2098\taccepted work recovered in the table exceeds the readout cost.\n2099\t\n2100\tCandidate position sources. We then compare four inexpensive position sources. On the matched Other and GigaSpeech diagnostics, draft attention reproduces 2–20% of\n2101\tcorrect-window behavior, and the speech-rate prior reproduces 17–32%. A previous-round frame reaches 64–66%, but\n2102\tits cost-inclusive result remains −5.3–−0.8%. Verification\n2103\tattention reaches 83–86% and is the only tested source that\n2104\tclears the cost-inclusive threshold.\n2105\t\n2106\tD.3\n2107\t\n2108\tMeasured position error. Matched cached-loop traces\n2109\tcompare draft attention with verification attention. Late-draft\n2110\tmedian error is 6, 10, and 21 frames on Clean, TED-LIUM,\n2111\tand GigaSpeech. Verification attention has a 2-frame median\n2112\tand a 6-frame 90th percentile. The result identifies verification\n2113\tattention as the more accurate measured source.\n2114\tEnd-to-end runtime correction. We apply the same correction at both Qwen scales to measure whether the position\n2115\testimate improves end-to-end speed after including its readout cost. Each run uses a matched uncorrected loop, and we\n2116\treport acceptance changes together with measured net speed.\n2117\tThe five-set net gains are +3.4% at 1.7B and +5.0% at\n2118\t\n2119\tTraining Correction with AnchorDraft\n2120\t\n2121\tAlignment objective. AnchorDraft adds a training-only\n2122\tloss that places the draft’s first-layer audio attention near the\n2123\tforced-alignment frame for the next token. The token and\n2124\tfeatu