access comparison uses LibriSpeech-only drafts.\n2269\tTo isolate the alignment objective, control and AnchorDraft use the same data, optimizer, training steps, seed, and\n2270\tdecoding protocol at each scale. We measure continuation,\n2271\ttarget output, and end-to-end speed with the inference graph\n2272\theld fixed.\n2273\tCorrection results. Continuation increases more than\n2274\trestart at both scales, while WER changes by at most 0.01\n2275\tpercentage points. Five-set speed gains span +6.4%–+7.1%\n2276\t\n2277\tacross the two 0.6B seeds and +3.9%–+5.0% across the\n2278\tthree 1.7B seeds. The result places the training effect in the\n2279\tpart of the rollout identified by the restart and continuation\n2280\tdiagnostic.\n2281\tCross-architecture boundary. The preregistered Voxtral\n2282\tcontrol uses a head whose position error remains stable during\n2283\tcontinuation. Position supervision changes its hard-domain\n2284\tequal-weighted continuation-acceptance average by −0.011.\n2285\tPost-hoc loss-weight sensitivity. We vary the alignmentloss weight to measure local sensitivity around the selected\n2286\tsetting. The sweep was run after the fixed holdout selected\n2287\tλ=0.1 and did not affect the selected loss weight, checkpoint,\n2288\tor test protocol.\n2289\tSpeed remains positive near the selected weight, peaks at\n2290\tλ=0.2, and falls at λ=0.3.\n2291\t\n2292\t\fModel\n2293\t\n2294\tSet\n2295\t\n2296\tQwen-1.7B\n2297\t\n2298\tClean\n2299\t\n2300\tQwen-1.7B\n2301\t\n2302\tOther\n2303\t\n2304\tQwen-1.7B\n2305\t\n2306\tTED-LIUM\n2307\t\n2308\tQwen-1.7B\n2309\t\n2310\tGigaSpeech\n2311\t\n2312\tQwen-1.7B\n2313\t\n2314\tFLEURS\n2315\t\n2316\tVoxtral-3B\n2317\t\n2318\tClean\n2319\t\n2320\tVoxtral-3B\n2321\t\n2322\tOther\n2323\t\n2324\tVoxtral-3B\n2325\t\n2326\tTED-LIUM\n2327\t\n2328\tVoxtral-3B\n2329\t\n2330\tGigaSpeech\n2331\t\n2332\tVoxtral-3B\n2333\t\n2334\tFLEURS\n2335\t\n2336\tCorrect − unrestricted\n2337\t\n2338\tPointer − unrestricted\n2339\t\n2340