r ↓\n2438\t\n2439\t∆a1 ↑ ∆ac2 ↑ Gross ↑\n2440\t\n2441\tClean\n2442\tOther\n2443\t1.7B TED-LIUM\n2444\tGigaSpeech\n2445\tFLEURS\n2446\t\n2447\t2.75 +0.0015 +0.010\n2448\t6.4 +0.003 +0.041\n2449\t7.1 +0.003 +0.046\n2450\t14.2 +0.006 +0.089\n2451\t12.7 +0.005 +0.076\n2452\t\n2453\tClean\n2454\tOther\n2455\t0.6B TED-LIUM\n2456\tGigaSpeech\n2457\tFLEURS\n2458\t\n2459\t3.5\n2460\t8.2\n2461\t9.0\n2462\t18.5\n2463\t15.2\n2464\t\n2465\t+0.7%\n2466\t+3.4%\n2467\t+3.8%\n2468\t+7.9%\n2469\t+6.8%\n2470\t\n2471\tNet [95% CI] ↑\n2472\t\n2473\t−0.4% [−1.0%, +0.2%]\n2474\t+2.3% [+1.1%, +3.5%]\n2475\t+2.7% [+1.4%, +4.0%]\n2476\t+6.8% [+5.1%, +8.5%]\n2477\t+5.7% [+4.0%, +7.4%]\n2478\t\n2479\t+0.002 +0.015 +0.9% −0.6% [−1.2%, +0.1%]\n2480\t+0.006 +0.075 +5.8% +4.3% [+2.9%, +5.7%]\n2481\t+0.005 +0.065 +5.0% +3.5% [+2.1%, +4.9%]\n2482\t+0.010 +0.140 +11.3% +9.8% [+8.1%, +11.5%]\n2483\t+0.009 +0.115 +9.6% +8.1% [+6.5%, +9.7%]\n2484\t\n2485\tTable 16: Runtime correction by target scale and evaluation set. Anchor error is in frames. Net gain is measured end to end. Gross\n2486\tsaving is an accounting estimate. Restart and continuation columns are absolute acceptance changes.\n2487\t\n2488\tD.4\n2489\t\n2490\tRelationship Between the Corrections\n2491\t\n2492\tCorrection overlap. If AnchorDraft reduces the same\n2493\tposition-sensitive error, a correct-position window should\n2494\trecover less accepted length after training. We measure this\n2495\tresidual gain with the window width, evaluation sets, and\n2496\taggregation fixed.\n2497\tThe absolute-reduction intervals are [0.030, 0.062] at 0.6B\n2498\tand [0.018, 0.037] at 1.7B. The remaining-gain intervals are\n2499\t[0.087, 0.130] and [0.063, 0.102]. The correct-position gain\n2500\tdecreases after training but remains positive at both scales.\n2501\tAnchorDraft therefore removes part, but not all, of the\n2502\terror recovered by the correct-position intervention. This\n2503\testablishes residual position-sensitive headroom; the stacked\n2504\texperiment below tests whether the runtime readout can use\n2505\tit.\n2506\t\n2507\tStacked correction. A final matched comparison applies\n2508\truntime correction after AnchorDraft training. At 1.7B, we\n2509\tcompare its absolute speedup with the control and AnchorDraft alone to measure the remaining runtime gain.\n2510\tRuntime correction adds +1.8% over AnchorDraft with\n2511\t95% CI [+0.9%, +2.7%]. The maximum absolute WER\n2512\tchange is 0.01 percentage points. The positive added gain\n2513\tshows that runtime correction still recovers work after training.\n2514\t\n2515\tE\n2516\t\n2517\tConfiguration, Alternatives, and\n2518\tDeployment Scope\n2519\t\n2520\tContinuation quality alone does not determine speed. This\n2521\tsection tests that boundary through draft configuration, training coverage, alternative draft changes, and serving conditions\n2522\tthat change the quality-cost balance.\n2523\t\n2524\t\fTwo-step\n2525\tWER (%) ↓ a1 ↑ ac2 ↑ length ↑ Latency ↓ Speed ↑\n2526\t\n2527\tTarget Seed Draft\n2528\t0.6B\n2529\t0.6B\n2530\t0.6B\n2531\t0.6B\n2532\t\n2533\t0\n2534\t0\n2535\t1\n2536\t1\n2537\t\n2538\tControl\n2539\tAnchorDraft\n2540\tControl\n2541\tAnchorDraft\n2542\t\n2543\t6.420 0.731 0.512\n2544\t6.420 0.754 0.628\n2545\t6.420 0.728 0.518\n2546\t6.430 0.747 0.623\n2547\t\n2548\t1.105\n2549\t1.228\n2550\t1.105\n2551\t1.212\n2552\t\n2553\t1.000\n2554\t0.934\n2555\t1.000\n2556\t0.940\n2557\t\n2558\t1.000\n2559\t1.071\n2560\t1.000\n2561\t1.064\n2562\t\n2563\t1.7B\n2564\t1.7B\n2565\t1.7B\n2566\t1.7B\n2567\t1.7B\n2568\t1.7B\n2569\t\n2570\t0\n2571\t0\n2572\t1\n2573\t1\n2574\t2\n2575\t2\n2576\t\n2577\tControl\n2578\tAnchorDraft\n2579\tControl\n2580\tAnchorDraft\n2581\tControl\n2582\tAnchorDraft\n2583\t\n2584\t5.606 0.816 0.679\n2585\t5.608 0.826 0.728\n2586\t5.606 0.816 0.679\n2587\t5.610 0.824 0.723\n2588\t5.606 0.814 0.676\n2589\t5.608 0.822 0.734\n2590\t\n2591\t1.371\n2592\t1.427\n2593\t1.370\n2594\t1.420\n2595\t1.364\n2596\t1.426\n2597\t\n2598\t1.000\n2599\t0.956\n2600\t1.000\n2601\t0.962\n2602\t1.000\n2603\t0.953\n2604\t\n2605\t1.000\n2606\t1.046\n2607\t1.000\n2608\t1.039\n2609\t1.000\n2610\t1.050\n2611\t\n2612\tTable 17: AnchorDraft and matched controls averaged over five evaluation sets. Two-step length is a1 (1 + ac2 ). Latency and\n2613\tspeed are normalized to the control for each seed.\n2614\t0.6B five-set speed gain ↑\n2615\t\n2616\tλ\n2617\t0.00\n2618\t0.05\n2619\t0.10\n2620\t0.20\n2621\t0.30\n2622\t\n2623\tE.2\n2624\t\n2625\t0.0%\n2626\t5.6%\n2627\t6.8%\n2628\t7.0%\n2629\t6.1%\n2630\t\n2631\tTable 18: Post-hoc five-set speed sensitivity to the alignmentloss weight at 0.6B.\n2632\t\n2633\ttokens or normalized cost\n2634\t\n2635\tL(K ) accepted length\n2636\t\n2637\tK ρ draft cost\n2638\t\n2639\tThe training-coverage experiment asks whether broader data\n2640\talone removes the correct-position gap. We compare cleanonly, three-set, and five-set drafts under the same official-split\n2641\tprotocol, then measure unrestricted accepted length and the\n2642\tadditional gain from a correct-position window.\n2643\tBroader training raises unrestricted accepted length, but a\n2644\tcorrect-position window still helps when the evaluation set\n2645\tis represented in training. Training coverage alone therefore\n2646\tdoes not remove the position gap.\n2647\t\n2648\tE.3\n2649\t\n2650\tControls for Alternative Explanations\n2651\t\n2652\tTraining coverage is evaluated in the preceding subsection.\n2653\tThe controls here test whether continuation loss can be\n2654\tresolved by token information, draft capacity, the training\n2655\tobjective, candidate coverage, or a relaxed output constraint.\n2656\tEach control changes one property of the default draft under\n2657\tits matched evaluation protocol. We compare its acceptance\n2658\tand speed with the corresponding default.\n2659\t\n2660\t1.25\n2661\t1.00\n2662\t0.75\n2663\t0.50\n2664\t1\n2665\t\n2666\t2\n2667\t\n2668\t3\n2669\t4\n2670\t5\n2671\tmaximum draft length K\n2672\t\n2673\t8\n2674\t\n2675\tFigure 7: Accepted length and per-round draft cost by maximum draft length K.\n2676\t\n2677\tE.1\n2678\t\n2679\tTraining Coverage\n2680\t\n2681\tDraft Length and Confidence Threshold\n2682\t\n2683\tLet ρ be the cost of one draft step relative to one target pass.\n2684\tA longer draft helps only when the added survival probability\n2685\tcovers its added drafting cost. The batch-one Qwen 1.7B\n2686\tmeasurement gives ρ = 0.0275. The real cached-loop sweep\n2687\tbelow measures that tradeoff directly.\n2688\tWith threshold 0.0, speed peaks at K=2 (1.332×). Threshold 0.3 shifts the measured peak to K=3 (1.365×). In both\n2689\tsweeps, accepted length saturates while draft cost continues\n2690\tto grow. These optima apply to the 80 Clean utterances and\n2691\tbatch-one implementation measured here.\n2692\t\n2693\tTree-decoder implementation check. This control tests\n2694\twhether proposing several token paths can improve continuation through wider candidate coverage. The draft builds a\n2695\tfixed candidate tree, and the target verifies all nodes in one\n2696\tpass with a tree attention mask. A one-branch tree reproduces\n2697\tthe linear decoder token for token. On the matched long-audio\n2698\ttest, the 11-node tree raises root hit rate from 0.76 to 0.88.\n2699\tUnder the tested budgets, the default one-layer crossentropy draft gives the best measured tradeoff among the\n2700\targmax-preserving alternatives. Added capacity, later feature\n2701\taccess, and wider candidate coverage do not by themselves\n2702\tresolve continuation loss in these comparisons.\n2703\t\n2704\tE.4\n2705\t\n2706\tDeployment Conditions\n2707\t\n2708\tThese measurements test whether the batch-one, short-form\n2709\ttiming result extends to other serving conditions. CUDA\n2710\tevents separate drafting and target verification, a microbenchmark varies batch size at fixed accepted length, and a real-loop\n2711\tsweep concatenates audio into longer inputs.