\t1.10×\n3082\t1.00×\n3083\t\n3084\tContinuation falls\n3085\tAdded cost removes the gain\n3086\t\n3087\tSoft KL objective\n3088\tScheduled sampling\n3089\t\n3090\tToken quality\n3091\tContinuation exposure\n3092\t\n3093\t0.99×\n3094\tNot timed\n3095\t\n3096\tTree draft\n3097\t\n3098\tCandidate coverage\n3099\t\n3100\t−0.037 continuation\n3101\tApproximately no continuation change\n3102\t−0.111 continuation\n3103\t+0.054 conditional continuation\n3104\tL changes by 1.36→1.70\n3105\t\n3106\tRelaxed tokens\n3107\t\n3108\tAcceptance\n3109\t\n3110\tSmall WER-neutral increase\n3111\t\n3112\tNarrow gain\n3113\t\n3114\tWorse than cross-entropy\n3115\tRestart and two-step survival\n3116\tfall\n3117\tWider verification removes\n3118\tthe gain\n3119\tChanges the output constraint\n3120\t\n3121\t1.60→1.56×\n3122\t\n3123\tTable 23: Alternative draft and verification changes under their matched evaluation protocols.\n3124\t\n3125\tF.1\n3126\t\n3127\tSame-Family Recognizer as Draft\n3128\t\n3129\tMatched setup. A complete recognizer can maintain its\n3130\town audio state, but its computation may erase the benefit\n3131\tof accepted tokens. We test this tradeoff with a Qwen3-ASR0.6B recognizer that drafts for the frozen 1.7B target in the\n3132\tsame real cached loop. Timing includes its audio encoder and\n3133\tprefill. The models share a tokenizer, and use exact-match\n3134\tgreedy verification. The output is target-greedy exact under\n3135\texact arithmetic; Section A reports the low-precision check.\n3136\tSet\n3137\t\n3138\tFull recognizer\n3139\t\n3140\tClean\n3141\t0.592 [0.577, 0.607]\n3142\tOther\n3143\t0.654 [0.638, 0.669]\n3144\tTED-LIUM 0.701 [0.690, 0.712]\n3145\tGigaSpeech 0.698 [0.687, 0.709]\n3146\tFLEURS\n3147\t0.657 [0.642, 0.672]\n3148\t\n3149\tShallow self-draft\n3150\t1.526\n3151\t1.517\n3152\t1.613\n3153\t1.449\n3154\t1.438\n3155\t\n3156\tTable 24: Speedup over autoregressive decoding on matched\n3157\tslices with K=4 and n=120 per evaluation set. Fullrecognizer brackets give paired 95% bootstrap intervals.\n3158\tProposal quality and cost. The full recognizer reaches\n3159\trestart acceptance 0.941–0.973 and continuation acceptance\n3160\t0.963–0.978, but adds 600M parameters and 1.46 GiB of\n3161\tstatic weights. The shallow self-draft uses 76.2M trainable\n3162\tparameters. The full recognizer remains slower than autoregressive decoding on all five sets, while the shallow self-draft\n3163\tremains faster. Better proposals alone do not offset the cost\n3164\tof a complete second recognizer in this setup.\n3165\tMatched timing. Both systems are timed serially on the\n3166\tsame idle A100-SXM4-40GB. Because the target dominates\n3167\tpeak allocation, we compare the additional static model\n3168\tweights rather than peak memory.\n3169\t\n3170\tF.2\n3171\t\n3172\tWhisper Cross-Architecture Check\n3173\t\n3174\tAudio access and continuation. We test whether the restart\n3175\tand continuation ordering transfers to Whisper. A real cached\n3176\tloop uses Whisper-large-v3 as the frozen target and compares\n3177\tmatched one-layer drafts with and without per-step audio\n3178\tcross-attention (Radford et al. 2023; Li et al. 2025a). SPGISpeech and VoxPopuli extend the evaluation beyond the five\n3179\tcore sets (O’Neill et al. 2021; Wang et al. 2021).\n3180\tThe draft with audio access is faster on all seven sets. Its\n3181\tmatched acceptance traces also show a modest restart change\n3182\tand a larger continuation change. The ordering therefore\n3183\textends to this Whisper implementation.\n3184\tOffline and real-loop measurement. To test whether fixedprefix acceptance predicts deployment behavior, we evaluate\n3185\tthe same Whisper draft with offline scoring, chained survival,\n3186\tand cached decoding. Offline accepted length is 9.4, chained\n3187\tsurvival gives 4.83, and the real cached loop gives 4.35.\n3188\tRestart acceptance is 0.92 offline and 0.635 in the loop.\n3189\tFixed-prefix scoring therefore overstates the work accepted\n3190\tduring deployment.\n3191\tRemaining restart error. The remaining Whisper restart\n3192\terror may come from position or token information. We\n3193\tcompare unrestricted and correct-position attention, then\n3194\tprobe two inputs to the draft against the target token. Restart\n3195\tacceptance is 0.597 with unrestricted attention and 0.529\n3196\twith the correct window. The target feature after combining\n3197\ttext and audio predicts the token at 0.948, while the compact\n3198\tdraft state reaches 0.534. These measurements point to token\n3199\tinformation, rather than audio position, as the main limitation\n3200\tin this restart comparison.\n3201\t\n3202\t\fSet\n3203\tLibriSpeech Clean\n3204\tLibriSpeech Other\n3205\tTED-LIUM\n3206\tSPGISpeech\n3207\tVoxPopuli\n3208\tGigaSpeech\n3209\tFLEURS\n3210\t\n3211\tWith audio speedup ↑\n3212\t\n3213\tWithout audio speedup ↑\n3214\t\n3215\tWER ↓\n3216\t\n3217\t2.75×\n3218\t2.31×\n3219\t2.25×\n3220\t2.35×\n3221\t2.04×\n3222\t2.01×\n3223\t1.59×\n3224\t\n3225\t1.03×\n3226\t1.01×\n3227\t1.03×\n3228\t0.99×\n3229\t0.97×\n3230\t0.98×\n3231\t0.93×\n3232\t\n3233\t2.5%\n3234\t4.7%\n3235\t5.8%\n3236\t4.0%\n3237\t13.5%\n3238\t11.4%\n3239\t6.3%\n3240\t\n3241\tTable 25: Whisper-large-v3 real cached loop with K=12 and n=100 per evaluation set. The draft is trained on LibriSpeech only.\n3242\t\n3243\tF.3\n3244\t\n3245\tRepository Measurements and Published\n3246\tSystems\n3247\t\n3248\tRepository measurements. The repository comparison\n3249\tuses a greedy Whisper implementation, except for the samefamily Qwen row. The rows differ in draft length and evaluation sample. They compare resource and output assumptions\n3250\trather than methods under identical conditions.\n3251\tAdditional model resources. The Whisper draft with audio\n3252\taccess adds 27M parameters and reuses the target encoder\n3253\toutput. The paired distilled draft adds 756M parameters and\n3254\truns its own audio encoder and prefill. In bfloat16, their\n3255\tadditional weights occupy approximately 0.05 and 1.41 GiB\n3256\tbefore activations and caches. The paired draft therefore adds\n3257\t28× as many draft parameters.\n3258\tPublished systems under original conditions. The published results below retain each system’s reported resources,\n3259\toutput constraint, hardware, and evaluation protocol.\n3260\t\n3261\t\fDesign\n3262\tPaired distilled draft\n3263\tSame-family 0.6B recognizer\n3264\tCTC or encoder draft\n3265\tText-tuned draft\n3266\tWhisper-Medusa\n3267\tAssisted generation\n3268\tDraft with audio access\n3269\t\n3270\tSingle model\n3271\t\n3272\tWER-neutral\n3273\t\n3274\tUses audio\n3275\t\n3276\tSpeedup ↑\n3277\t\n3278\t✗\n3279\t✗\n3280\t✓\n3281\t✓\n3282\t✓\n3283\t✗\n3284\t✓\n3285\t\n3286\t✓\n3287\t✓\n3288\t✓\n3289\t✓\n3290\t✗\n3291\t✓\n3292\t✓\n3293\t\n3294\t✓\n3295\t✓\n3296\t✓\n3297\t✗\n3298\t✗\n3299\t✓\n3300\t✓\n3301\t\n3302\t3.98–4.10×\n3303\t0.59–0.70×\n3304\t1.53×\n3305\tApproximately 1.5×\n3306\t0.85×\n3307\t0.87×\n3308\t2.75×\n3309\t\n3310\tTable 26: Repository ASR drafting measurements under the Whisper implementation. The same-family recognizer row uses\n3311\tQwen.\n3312\t\n3313\tSystem\n3314\t\n3315\tExtra resource\n3316\t\n3317\tReported output condition\n3318\t\n3319\tReported result\n3320\t\n3321\tWhisperKit (Orhon\n3322\tet al. 2025; Cheng et al.\n3323\t2024)\n3324\tSpecASR (Wei et al.\n3325\t2025)\n3326\tIBM CTC speculation (Saon et al. 2026)\n3327\tWhisperMedusa\n3328\t(SegalFeldman et al. 2024)\n3329\tToken-Map (Ho et al.\n3330\t2025)\n3331\t\n3332\tRecurrent drafter\n3333\t\n3334\tReported lossless\n3335\t\n3336\t2.24× on v3 and 1.25× on turbo\n3337\t\n3338\tCompanion ASR\n3339\t\n3340\tWER-preserving\n3341\t\n3342\t3.04–3.79×\n3343\t\n3344\tCTC branch\n3345\t\n3346\t4.4× inverse real-time factor\n3347\t\n3348\tMultiple heads\n3349\t\n3350\tRelaxed with 12% relative WER increase\n3351\tMinimal WER impact reported\n3352\t\n3353\tN-gram map\n3354\t\n3355\tNo accuracy loss reported\n3356\t\n3357\t1.27–1.37×\n3358\t\n3359\tApproximately 50% latency reduction\n3360\t\n3361\tTable 27: Published ASR acceleration systems under their original resource, output, and hardware conditions.\n3362\t\n3363\t\f\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "372e2c33-5831-490d-9a02-69da930385ec", "uuid": "9b9f7b4c-e238-4d8b-835a-63000c40aa34", "timestamp": "2026-08-14T06:33:30.072Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper_2608.12703v1.txt", "content": "Alignment Drift in Single-Model Speculative Decoding for ASR:\nMechanism, Correction, and Cost\nXinyu Wang1,2∗ , Huapeng Zhou1 , Ziyu Zhao2 , Silin Meng1 ,\nKe Bai1 , Dongming Shen1 , Xiao-Wen Chang2 , Alex Smola1\n1\n\n2\n\nBoson AI\nMcGill University\n\narXiv:2608.12703v1 [cs.SD] 13 Aug 2026\n\nAbstract\nSpeculative decoding speeds up generation by letting a cheap\ndraft propose several tokens that a target model checks in\none pass. In the single-model form, the draft is a lightweight\nmodule attached to the target rather than a separate model.\nApplying this design to Automatic Speech Recognition (ASR)\nintroduces an extra problem. The draft can read the whole\naudio at every step, yet its proposals get worse as it runs on its\nown. Access is not localization. The accepted text keeps the\ntranscript position explicit, but the draft must also track the\nchanging audio position. In the primary matched comparison,\nper-step audio access changes the first proposal modestly\nbut roughly doubles later-proposal acceptance. Fixed-width\nwindows show that the audio position explains part of this\ngap. A correctly placed window recovers continuation, while\nan equally narrow window at the wrong position reduces\nit. Late-draft median error reaches 21 frames in the hardest\nreported condition, while target attention during verification\nstays within a 2-frame median. We test two ways to reduce\nthis drift. The first reads the audio position from verification\nattention and uses it to guide the next draft round. It saves\ntime only when the extra accepted tokens offset the readout\ncost. The second is AnchorDraft, which teaches the draft\nto track the audio position during training without changing\nthe inference graph. The trained draft improves end-to-end\nspeed at both tested target scales. These results show that ASR\nself-speculation depends on token prediction, audio-position\ntracking, and draft cost.\n\n1\n\nIntroduction\n\nLarge Language Models (LLMs) use AutoRegressive (AR)\ndecoding, which requires one target-model pass per generated\ntoken. Speculative decoding reduces the number of target\npasses while returning the target’s greedy output under exact\narithmetic. A cheaper draft proposes several tokens, and\nthe target checks them in one forward pass and accepts the\nlongest matching prefix (Leviathan, Kalman, and Matias\n2023; Chen et al. 2023; Xia et al. 2023). Text systems obtain\nthese proposals from a smaller model, auxiliary prediction\nheads, or early target layers (Cai et al. 2024; Li et al. 2025b,a;\nZhang et al. 2024; Elhoushi et al. 2024). Their speed depends\non both the accepted draft length and the cost of producing\nit (Yan, Agarwal, and Venkataraman 2025).\n∗\n\nEmail: xinyu@boson.ai\n\nExtending this design to Automatic Speech Recognition\n(ASR) adds a moving audio input to this problem. Paired\nsystems use a second recognizer that follows the audio independently (Gandhi, von Platen, and Rush 2023; Wei et al.\n2025), but require serving another recognizer. We instead\nattach a lightweight draft to the target. It reuses the tokenizer,\nencoded audio, and decoder cache while the target is idle.\nThe draft must follow the audio and cost less than the target\npasses it replaces.\nTo locate where proposals fail, we split each speculative\nround. The restart is the first proposal after verification. Later\ndraft-only proposals are continuation proposals. Per-step audio access changes restart acceptance modestly in our primary\nmatched comparison but roughly doubles continuation acceptance. The main loss develops while the draft runs without\nthe target.\nDraft cost is a separate constraint. Drafts with per-step audio\naccess reach 1.4–1.7× end-to-end speedup with WER close\nto autoregressive decoding. A same-family full recognizer\nproduces almost perfect proposals but is slower than autoregressive decoding. Useful proposals must reduce enough\ntarget work to cover their cost.\nThe restart and continuation split points to information that\nchanges between target passes. The accepted prefix records\nwhich tokens have been generated. Audio adds a second\nrequirement. The draft reads the entire encoded audio at\nevery step, yet it must still find the frame needed for its next\ntoken. We call this frame the audio anchor. The text position\nadvances one token at a time, while the audio anchor moves\nby a variable number of frames because token duration varies.\nLocation-aware and monotonic attention address a related\nmoving-position problem in conventional speech decoding\n(Chorowski et al. 2015; Chiu and Raffel 2018). Here, the\nposition must be maintained by a shallow draft between\ntarget passes. We use alignment drift for the growing distance\nbetween the draft’s attention and the audio anchor during later\ndraft steps.\nContinuation decay could also reflect weak token features,\nconfidence errors, or limited draft capacity. We isolate audio\nposition by fixing the visible window width and changing\nonly its center. A correct-position window recovers part of the\nlost continuation, while a wrong-position window reduces it.\nThe official-split replication gives the same ordering. Audio\nposition explains part of the gap, but not every rejection.\n\n\fInternal probes separate this position from next-token information. In the hardest reported condition, late-draft median\nerror reaches 21 frames, compared with a 2-frame median\nduring target verification.\nThese measurements connect continuation loss to a position that verification still estimates accurately. We test two\ncorrections. The runtime correction uses verification attention\nto position the next draft round. AnchorDraft adds position\nsupervision during training without changing inference. Both\nimprove continuation on the tested Qwen checkpoints. Their\nend-to-end benefit depends on whether the accepted work\nthey recover covers their cost, so all latency results use the\nreal cached loop.\n\n2\n\nRelated Work\n\nText Speculative Decoding. Lossless speculative decoding\nverifies several tokens from a cheaper draft and returns the\ntarget’s greedy sequence under exact arithmetic (Leviathan,\nKalman, and Matias 2023; Chen et al. 2023). The same\ndraft-and-verify pattern also applies to sequence-to-sequence\ngeneration (Xia et al. 2023). Single-model methods either\nattach proposal heads (Cai et al. 2024; Li et al. 2025b,a) or\ndraft from early target layers (Zhang et al. 2024; Elhoushi et al.\n2024). These designs avoid serving a separate draft model,\nbut their speed still depends on the work accepted per round\nand the cost of drafting (Yan, Agarwal, and Venkataraman\n2025). MARS instead changes verification by relaxing strict\ntoken matching when the target margin is small (Song et al.\n2026). We keep greedy verification fixed and study the draft\nstate between target passes.\nSpeculative ASR. ASR systems draw proposals from a\nsecond recognizer (Wei et al. 2025), an encoder-attached\nlightweight decoder (Lim et al. 2025), connectionist temporal classification outputs (Saon et al. 2026), or transcript\nmaps (Ho et al. 2025). Distil-Whisper provides a smaller\npaired recognizer, while Whisper-Medusa attaches parallel\nprediction heads to the recognizer (Gandhi, von Platen, and\nRush 2023; Segal-Feldman et al. 2024). Partially autoregressive masking and anticipatory recognition change the search\nor input contract (Okabe and Yamamoto 2025; Yusuf et al.\n2024). WhisperKit evaluates ReDrafter but removes it from\none final configuration when drafting overhead outweighs its\nbenefit (Orhon et al. 2025; Cheng et al. 2024). Our setting\ninstead uses a shallow draft attached to the target and asks\nwhy it loses proposals while reading fixed audio memory.\nSpeech Alignment and Probes. Attention-based speech\nrecognizers learn an input-output alignment (Chan et al.\n2016). Location-aware attention, monotonic attention, and\ncoverage objectives constrain that alignment (Chorowski et al.\n2015; Raffel et al. 2017; Chiu and Raffel 2018; Tachibana,\nUenoyama, and Aihara 2018; See, Liu, and Manning 2017).\nAnchorDraft applies this idea only to the shallow draft’s\naudio attention. The frozen target remains unchanged, and\nthe outcome is speculative acceptance and latency rather than\nalignment quality itself. Linear probes and logit-lens analysis\ncan locate readable information inside the decoder, but probe\naccuracy alone does not establish causal use (Tenney, Das,\n\nand Pavlick 2019; Hewitt and Liang 2019; nostalgebraist\n2020). The Supplementary Material (Supp.) gives broader\nsystem comparisons and timing decomposition in Section F\nand Sections E.4 and F.3.\n\n3\n\nPreliminaries and Evaluation Setup\n\nSpeculative Loop. In each round, the draft proposes up to\nK tokens, where K is the maximum draft length. The frozen\ntarget checks the block in one pass and accepts the longest\nprefix that matches its greedy output. Under exact arithmetic,\nthis verification rule returns the same sequence as targetgreedy decoding. The first proposal after verification is the\nrestart. Proposals before the next verification are continuation\nproposals. Let sk be the probability that the first k proposals\nare accepted, with s0 =1. Conditional acceptance at depth\nk is ack = sk /sk−1 . We report a1 as restart acceptance\nand ac2 as continuation acceptance. The mean number of\nPK\naccepted draft tokens is L = k=1 sk . End-to-end speedup\nis autoregressive greedy wall-clock divided by speculative\nwall-clock on the same utterances. Word Error Rate (WER)\nmeasures recognition error. Anchor error is the absolute\ndistance between an attention peak and its reference audio\nframe. It is reported on the post-stride audio grid or in\nmilliseconds. One grid frame is approximately 80 ms for the\nQwen and Voxtral analyses in this paper. Acceptance and\naccepted-length differences are absolute unless marked as\nrelative. We use speed gain for the relative percentage change\nfrom a matched control. WER is reported in percent, and\nWER changes are in percentage points. Qwen aggregates\nweight the five evaluation sets equally within each seed and\nthen weight seeds equally.\nNumerical Output Agreement. The verification rule is\nlossless under exact arithmetic, but the measured lowprecision loop is not always token-for-token identical to\na separately computed target trace. Across 150 utterances\nper set, token agreement is 0.9705–0.9966, utterance agreement is 0.9267–0.9933, and WER differences are small and\nsign-mixed. Full-precision recomputation attributes the differences to low-margin decisions. Supp. tables 4 and 5 gives\nthe complete check.\nModels and Protocol. The draft follows the direct-tokenprediction and multi-layer feature design of the Extrapolation\nAlgorithm for Greater Language-model Efficiency (EAGLE3) (Li et al. 2025a). We add cross-attention from each draft\nstep to the frozen audio encoder. We train drafts for two\nQwen3-ASR scales and Voxtral-Mini, with Whisper as a\ncross-architecture comparison (Shi et al. 2026; Liu et al.\n2025; Radford et al. 2023). Evaluation uses LibriSpeech\nclean and other, TED-LIUM, GigaSpeech, and FLEURS\n(Panayotov et al. 2015; Hernandez et al. 2018; Chen et al.\n2021; Conneau et al. 2022). The matched feasibility drafts\nuse LibriSpeech train-clean-100. The main causal results use\nofficial training data, development-only selection, and official\ntest sets. Supp. Section A and Section C.1 give the partitions,\nsample sizes, and matched configurations. Earlier checkpoints\ntrained on prefixes from test-partition slices are retained only\nas diagnostics in Supp. Section C. The Qwen experiments\n\n\f(a)\n\nwith audio\n\n(b)\nno audio\n\n0.75\n\nverify\n\nk =1\n\nk =2\n\nacceptance\n\ncorrect anchor\n\n0.50\n\nk =3\n\nverify\n\n2 frames\n\ndraft attention\n\nabout 2×\n\n0.25\n\ndrift\n\n0.00\nrestart\n\ncontinuation\n\n(c)\nverify\n\n···\n\ndraft\n\n···\n\n6–21 frames\n\nreset\n\naccess 6= localization\n\nFigure 1: Overview of alignment drift. (a) Per-step audio access changes restart modestly and separates continuation. (b) Draft\nposition drifts between target verifications and resets after verification. (c) Late-draft and verification anchor errors.\n\nused for the main timing results run on one NVIDIA A10080GB GPU at batch size one in bfloat16. Autoregressive and\nspeculative timing use the same utterances and start from\nthe waveform, including preprocessing, audio encoding, and\ncached decoding. The separate system comparisons report\ntheir own hardware and timing protocol.\nReal Cached-Loop Measurement. Fixed-prefix scoring\nomits errors that enter later draft inputs. We therefore compare\noffline scoring, chained survival on fixed prefixes, and the\nreal cached loop under one decoding setup. Mean accepted\nlength falls from 9.4 under offline scoring to 4.83 under\nchained survival and 4.35 in the real loop. Restart acceptance\nfalls from 0.92 offline to 0.635 in the loop. Unless explicitly\nlabeled as offline or chained diagnostics, reported acceptance\nand latency results use the real cached loop. Supp. Section A\nand Section F.2 give the decoding parameters and the full\ncomparison of measurement protocols.\nUncertainty. Unless stated otherwise, reported 95% confidence intervals (CIs) use paired utterance bootstrap resampling, with matched runs kept in each draw. They condition\non the trained checkpoints, so we report seed values or ranges\nseparately. Depth and state analyses use 5,000 draws, while\ncorrection timing uses 10,000 and treats timing repetitions\nas blocks. The artifact includes the mapping rules, bootstrap\ncode, figure data, and run metadata.\nThe experiments first locate failure within the rollout, then\nchange only the audio position, and finally test whether the\nrecovered work covers the cost of correction.\n\n4\n\nAlignment Drift\n\nWe first locate the acceptance loss within each speculative\nround and show that audio-position error grows over the same\nsteps. We then validate the anchor measure against an independent forced aligner and relate its error to proposal outcomes\nacross draft depth. Finally, we change only the position of\na fixed-width audio window to test whether position affects\ncontinuation.\nRestart and Continuation. Per-step audio access can affect\neither the first proposal after verification or the draft’s ability\nto sustain later steps. These cases produce different acceptance\n\nAudio\n\na1 ↑\n\nContinuation\nac2 ↑\n\nSpeedup ↑\n\nQwen 1.7B\n\n✗\n✓\n\n0.60–0.73\n0.62–0.76\n\n0.25–0.32\n0.54–0.58\n\n1.14–1.30×\n1.41–1.55×\n\nVoxtral 3B\n\n✗\n✓\n\n0.47–0.62\n0.57–0.69\n\n0.22–0.25\n0.71–0.78\n\n0.95–1.08×\n1.42–1.69×\n\nTarget\n\nTable 1: Restart acceptance, continuation acceptance, and\nspeedup for matched drafts with and without per-step audio\ncross-attention. Each pair shares the model, layer budget,\nLibriSpeech train-clean-100 data, and decoding loop. Ranges\ngive the minimum and maximum over five evaluation sets.\n\nprofiles. We compare matched draft variants with and without\nper-step audio cross-attention. Both drafts use the same target\nfeatures, layer budget, training setup, and decoding loop. Only\nthe draft with audio access cross-attends to the frozen audio\nrepresentation at every draft step.\nThe matched drafts differ little at restart but separate during\ncontinuation on every evaluation set. The audio-conditioned\ndrafts also remain faster than autoregressive decoding. The\nuseful effect of audio access therefore develops between\ntarget passes rather than at the first proposal alone. Supp.\ntable 6 gives the five set-level aggregates. A separate Whisper\nexperiment follows the same ordering under an earlier timing\nprotocol and is reported in Supp. Section F.2.\nCost Boundary. A stronger draft tests whether complete\nASR state can prevent continuation decay. A Qwen3-ASR0.6B recognizer drafts for the 1.7B target with a shared\ntokenizer, but it runs its own encoder and decoder at every\nstep. It maintains nearly perfect restart and continuation acceptance, yet reaches only 0.59–0.70× autoregressive speed.\nThis control separates position maintenance from cost. The\nrecognizer follows the audio, but its accepted tokens do not\nrepay its computation.\nOfficial-Split Replication. To check whether the pattern\ndepends on the feasibility checkpoint, we repeat the comparison on a speaker-stratified official split. Two independently\nseeded drafts with per-step audio and one matched no-audio\n\n\fcontrol use the same training and evaluation protocol. The\ncontinuation difference remains larger than the restart difference on every evaluation set. Its magnitude is smaller than in\nthe feasibility runs, but its direction is unchanged. This replication shows that the restart and continuation ordering does\nnot depend on the feasibility checkpoint. Supp. Section C.1\nreports the matched results.\nThe feasibility, official-split, and training-correction experiments use different training sets and checkpoints. We compare\ndirections across these experiments but do not treat their speed\nvalues as repeated measurements of one configuration.\nDrift over Draft Depth. The restart comparison locates the\nloss between target passes but does not show how it develops.\nWe therefore follow four draft steps on two official-split\nseeds and all five evaluation sets. Anchor error rises while\nsurvival falls, with the same slope directions in every seed\nand set. Cumulative survival sk tracks the fraction of initial\nrounds that accept through depth k. Conditional acceptance\nack shows where the curves separate, but at later depths it\ncompares a different survivor population in each condition.\nThe position intervention below reports both survival and\nconditional acceptance.\nValidating the Anchor Measure. Our analysis treats the\npeak of the draft’s audio cross-attention as its current audio position. We compare this measure with token frames\nfrom the independent Massively Multilingual Speech Forced\nAligner (MMS-FA) (Pratap et al. 2024). The two positions\nare correlated both across and within utterances on every\nevaluated set (fig. 2b). The higher Voxtral correlations do not\nimply lower absolute error. Its attention peak is systematically late relative to MMS-FA. Removing the set-level offset\nsubstantially reduces the error. We therefore treat Voxtral as\na stable but offset boundary case, not as an example of exact\nlocalization. The within-utterance agreement rules out utterance duration as the only explanation, so we use the attention\npeak as an operational position measure. A paired probe at\nthe same restart events reads more position information after\nverification than from the free-running draft state. The layer\nand ridge strength are fixed on separate development data.\nThe probe shows where position is readable but does not\nestablish causal use. Supp. tables 7 and 9 gives the anchor\nvalidation and paired probe protocol.\nAnchor Error and Proposal Outcome. Agreement with\nthe independent aligner establishes that the attention peak\ntracks audio position. We next compare accepted and rejected\nproposals at each draft depth. Large errors are associated with\nrejection at restart, but the accepted and rejected distributions\noverlap later in the rollout (fig. 2c). Anchor error is therefore\nrelated to proposal failure without explaining every rejection.\nThe intervention below tests whether changing window position changes continuation acceptance. Supp. table 12 gives\nthe depth-resolved values.\nPosition Interventions. The prefix remains explicit during\na speculative round, while the relevant audio frame moves with\nevery draft step. A correct-position window should recover\ncontinuation if losing this position causes the observed decay.\nAn equally restrictive wrong-position window provides the\n\nOutcome\ns2\ns3\nL\n\nC−U\n\nW−U\n\nC−W\n\n+0.105\n−0.122\n+0.227\n[+0.099, +0.113] [−0.129, −0.115] [+0.219, +0.236]\n+0.083\n−0.039\n+0.122\n[+0.077, +0.089] [−0.043, −0.035] [+0.116, +0.128]\n+0.262\n−0.370\n+0.632\n[+0.248, +0.276] [−0.386, −0.356] [+0.612, +0.653]\n\nTable 2: Official-split position-intervention effects over two\nseeds and five evaluation sets. Entries are changes in sk\nP4\nand L =\nk=1 sk with utterance-cluster bootstrap 95%\nconfidence intervals. C, U, and W denote correct, unrestricted,\nand the prespecified shifted window.\n\nmatched control. MMS-FA supplies a reference audio frame\nfor each token, using the anchor measure validated in Section 4.\nWe compare five attention conditions. Only the center of the\ndraft’s cross-attention mask changes. Target verification, the\naccepted prefix, draft weights, and window width remain\nfixed. The unrestricted condition has no mask. The oracle\nwindow is centered on the current reference frame, while\nthe pointer window extrapolates from previously verified\nframes. The random condition samples an unrelated center.\nThe shift condition applies a fixed offset to the reference center.\nRandom and shift are therefore equally wide wrong-position\ncontrols. Oracle tests the effect of a correct current position.\nPointer tests whether positions from earlier verification steps\ncan guide the next round. Both are offline interventions, not\ndeployment sources. Supp. Sections A and C give the window\ngeometry, width controls, and full intervention matrix.\nThese conditions yield four comparisons. Wrong-position\nversus unrestricted attention measures sensitivity to position. Correct-center versus unrestricted attention measures\nrecoverable acceptance. Correct-center versus an equally\nwide wrong-position window separates position from window\nwidth. The errors that remain under correct positioning show\nhow much of the failure has another source.\nMatched Effect of Window Position. The official-split\nreplication provides the main causal check. All three arms\nuse the same utterances, draft weights, and target verifier. At\ndepth two, the correct-versus-shifted conditional-acceptance\ncontrast is +0.254 with 95% CI [+0.241, +0.268] (fig. 2a).\nThe complete depth curves show where this result holds.\nCorrect positioning has a positive conditional-acceptance\neffect through depth three. At depth four, the correct-versuswrong interval includes zero. Only 1.2% of unrestricted\nrounds reach that step, so the last estimate has little support.\nSupp. table 12 reports survival, conditional acceptance, anchor\nerror, and matched contrasts at all four depths.\nUnlike conditional acceptance, sk and L start from the\ninitial round population. Correct centering raises both measures, while the shifted window lowers them (table 2). The\ndirection is unchanged for either seed on every evaluation\nset. Audio position therefore affects continuation. Survival\nremains below one under correct centering, leaving a residual\nerror for other sources. Together with the independent anchor\nvalidation and depth trend, this result supports alignment\n\n\f+0.254\n0.2 +0.144\n\ncl\not\ntd\n\n0.0\n−0.2\n\n(b) Attention tracks position\n\ngg\n-0.111\nC−U\n\nW−U\n\nfl\nC−W\n\n(c) Survival over draft depth\ncumulative survival sk\n\ncontinuation change\n\n(a) Official position contrast\n\n0.0\n0.4\n0.8\ncorrelation with aligner\nshuffled\n\nattention\n\n0.6\n0.4\n0.2\n0.0\n\n1\n\n2\n3\ndraft depth\ncorrect\nunrestricted\n\n4\nwrong\n\nFigure 2: Three measurements of audio position. (a) Depth-two conditional-acceptance contrasts on matched official-split\nexamples with two seeds and n=120 utterances per evaluation set. Error bars show paired 95% confidence intervals. C, U, and W\ndenote correct, unrestricted, and shifted windows. (b) Correlation between the draft attention peak and an independent forced\naligner, with shuffled controls. (c) Cumulative proposal survival under the three attention conditions.\n\ndrift as one source of continuation loss. Supp. table 12 gives\nthe official matched comparison and the proposal-level decomposition. The correct-position gain persists over a broad\nintermediate range of window widths. Narrow windows exclude the pointer’s own error, while wide windows re-admit\ndistractors. Supp. Section A reports the sweep.\nDiagnostic Boundaries. The causal result does not imply\nthat every draft benefits from position correction. Two measurements determine its scope. Window sensitivity is the\nacceptance gap between correct and wrong centers. Drift is\nthe growth of anchor error under unrestricted attention.\nThe official-split drafts establish position sensitivity\nthrough depth three and replicate the restart-continuation\npattern. Earlier diagnostics show why window sensitivity and\ndrift must remain separate. One 0.6B checkpoint responds\nweakly to every window, while its official-split counterpart\nhas a correct-versus-wrong gap across two seeds. A sharedslice replay shows that the response depends strongly on the\ntrained checkpoint. We test one possible cause by retraining\nthe official draft with three levels of Gaussian noise added\nto its training features. The correct-versus-wrong contrast\nremains similar at later draft depths, so feature noise does not\naccount for the checkpoint gap under this training recipe. Supp.\nSection C.1, Section C, and table 14 gives these diagnostic\ncomparisons.\nThe Voxtral diagnostic supplies a second boundary. It\ndistinguishes correct from wrong window centers, yet a\nrestrictive window can reduce acceptance even at the correct\nposition. A useful correction therefore requires both position\nsensitivity and loss of useful position information during freerunning continuation. These earlier diagnostics define the\nscope of the official result rather than serving as its primary\nevidence.\n\nPosition Information inside the Decoder. The window\nintervention changes model input, but it does not show where\nthe decoder carries position information. We therefore train\nheld-out linear probes on each decoder layer. The probe\npredicts the forced-alignment frame after removing the withinutterance trend with token rank. Anchor predictability peaks\nin intermediate layers and weakens toward the output across\nboth Qwen scales and Voxtral. The paired test in Section 4\ncompares proposal-time and post-verification states at the\nsame restart events. These probes show where audio position\nis readable, not whether the decoder uses it causally (Hewitt\nand Liang 2019). The matched window experiment tests\nits effect on continuation. Supp. figs. 4b and 5 and table 9\ngives the probe definitions, next-token curves, and held-out\nintervals.\n\nAlternative Draft Changes. The final mechanism experiment tests simpler changes to the draft. Each alternative\nchanges one property of the default draft and is measured\nunder its own matched real-loop protocol. Later target layers\nchange continuation by −0.037, a two-layer draft by ≈ 0, and\nsoft KL training by −0.111. Their measured speed remains\n0.99×–1.10×. Scheduled sampling improves continuation\nby +0.054, while tree drafting increases accepted length\nbut reduces speed from 1.60→1.56×. None improves the\nquality-cost tradeoff. Broader official-split training improves\nthe unrestricted draft, but a correct-position window still\nrecovers accepted length. Supp. Sections E.2 to E.3 report\nthe matched protocols and full results.\nThe forced-alignment pointer remains an offline intervention. Section 5 next tests a runtime correction that uses information available during decoding and a training correction\nthat leaves the inference graph fixed.\n\n\f5\n\nCorrection and Cost\n\nTarget\n\nThe position interventions identify a recoverable part of the\ncontinuation loss. A practical correction must recover enough\naccepted work to offset its cost. We study two approaches.\nThe runtime approach obtains a new position from each\nverification pass. AnchorDraft instead teaches the draft to\nmaintain its position during training.\nCost Condition. Target verification accounts for 86–89%\nof Qwen speculative wall-clock, while drafting accounts for\n6.3–9.0%. A correction has room to help, but its gain must\ncover its own cost. Runtime correction improves latency when\n∆L\n∆Ccorr\n≳\n,\n1+L\nKρ + 1\n\n(1)\n\nwhere ∆L is the increase in accepted draft length, ∆Ccorr\nis the correction cost relative to one target verification, and\nρ is the cost of one draft step on the same scale. A matched\nbatch-one Qwen 1.7B measurement gives ρ = 0.0275.\nWe use this relation as a screening rule. Position must affect\nprediction, the draft must lose useful position information\nduring continuation, and the recovered accepted length must\ncover the readout cost. Across the 19 settings in fig. 3c, the\nmeasured result becomes positive only after the recovered\nwork clears this cost. The rule also matches the direction\nof 10/10 deployed evaluation-set point estimates across the\ntwo Qwen targets. This tests the sign, not the size, of the\nspeed gain. Voxtral fails the second condition because its\nposition error grows modestly while continuation remains\nhigh. Supp. Section E.4 and Sections C and E give the\ntiming decomposition, acceptance results, and evaluation-set\nintervals.\nCandidate Runtime Signals. The intervention uses forced\nalignment, which is unavailable during normal decoding. We\ntherefore ask whether the loop already contains a usable\nposition estimate. We compare draft attention, speech-rate\nextrapolation, a delayed position from the previous round,\nand verification attention. A usable source must be accurate\nand current after each target pass.\nA speech-rate prior reproduces only 17–32% of the correctwindow behavior. The previous-round position reproduces\n64–66% but changes speed by −5.3–−0.8%. Verification\nattention reproduces 83–86% and gives a +3.4% five-set\nmean speed gain. It is the only tested source that is both\ncurrent and cost-effective, although its clean interval includes\nzero.\nVerification-Attention Correction. The target’s audio attention during verification provides a fresh estimate on the\ntested Qwen checkpoints. We average attention over heads at\na layer selected on development data and use its peak to center\nthe next draft window. The implementation reads the existing\ncache, uses no forced alignment, and adds no target forward\npass. The same rule selects layer 21 for two independently\ntrained 1.7B drafts.\nIn matched traces, verification reduces a late-draft error\nof up to 21 frames to a 2-frame median, and the next restart\nremains close to that position. After charging the readout\ncost, the 1.7B correction improves the five-set mean. Its\n\n∆a1 /∆ac2 ↑\n\nSpeed [CI] ↑ Set range ↑\n\n0.6B +0.021/+0.111 +6.8% [+5.8%, +7.7%] 4.5–9.4%\n1.7B +0.009/+0.050 +4.5% [+3.55%, +5.57%] 2.7–6.2%\n\nTable 3: AnchorDraft changes from matched controls over\nfive equal-weighted evaluation sets. Set ranges contain the\nper-set mean speed gains.\nobserved seed-level gains span +3.0%–+3.8%, while the\nclean interval includes zero. At 0.6B, the corresponding seed\nrange is +4.7%–+5.4%. Supp. table 16 and Section C.1\nreport the layer rule, readout cost, acceptance recovery, and\nevaluation-set timing. The reset and timing result show that\nthis position is current enough to guide the next rollout.\nDeployment Scope. The runtime result above uses batchone, short-form requests. We vary batch size and audio\nduration to test how far that result extends. Target verification\namortizes faster than drafting as batch size grows, so the\ndraft-to-verification cost ratio rises from 0.0275 at batch one\nto 0.1274 at batch 64. A separate real-loop sweep shows that\nspeedup falls as audio duration grows from roughly 18 to\n104 seconds. The reported gains therefore describe short,\nbatch-one requests in the measured implementation. Supp.\nSection E.4 gives both sweeps.\nAnchorDraft Training Objective. Runtime correction\nreads and passes a new position after every verification.\nAnchorDraft instead teaches the existing draft to maintain\nthat position during training. It applies a Gaussian guidedattention target (Tachibana, Uenoyama, and Aihara 2018) to\nthe shallow draft rather than the frozen target. Each training\nexample uses the same token sequence, target features, and\nencoded audio as the control. Forced alignment maps the next\ntoken at position t to frame ct . The first draft layer produces\nattention At,hj from head h to audio\nPindex j. We average\nover heads to obtain Āt (j) = H −1 h At,hj and match it\nto the normalized Gaussian qt (j) ∝ exp[−(j − ct )2 /(2σ 2 )].\nUnaligned token positions and padded audio keys are masked.\nThe objective is\nL = Ltok + 0.5Lfeat + λLanchor ,\n\n(2)\n\nwhere Lanchor is cross-entropy between Āt and qt , with σ > 0\nand λ ≥ 0.\nTraining Protocol. Optimization updates only the draft.\nWe select λ=0.1 and σ=5 on a fixed holdout and train\nboth scales on the same 3,933-utterance mixture of official\ntraining-set data. The aligner and auxiliary loss are absent at\ninference, so AnchorDraft has the same decoding graph as\nits control. Target verification still accepts only target-greedy\ntokens. Supp. Sections D.3 and E.1 give the timestamp pairing,\nmasking rules, selected settings, and sensitivity sweeps.\nTraining Results. This experiment tests whether position\nsupervision improves the continuation steps identified in\nSection 4. Controls match the data, optimization, seed, and\ndecoding graph.\nTable 3 shows that the gain is concentrated in continuation rather than restart. Speed improves on every measured\n\n\f6\n4\n2\n\n10\n0\n\n0\n−2\n\n−10\ner\n\nan h ED iga RS\ncle ot T G EU\nFL\nlow drift\nmedium drift\nhigh drift\n\n20\ncost-inclusive gain (%)\n\n20\nchange\n\ncost-inclusive gain (%)\n\n8\n\n(b) recovery and cost\n\nn er D\na\nclea oth TE Gig\nacceptance recovery\ncost-inclusive latency\n\n(c) runtime conditions\n\n10\n\n0\n\n−10\n\n(d) shared correction\n0.200\n\npays\ncorrect-position gain\n\n(a) 1.7B deployed correction\n\n0.175\n\n0.155\n\n0.150\n0.6B 30%\n0.109\n\n0.125\n0.100\n\n1.7B 24%\n0.111\n\n0.075\n0.00\n0.25\ncorrect-position gain L\n\nQwen-1.7B\nQwen-0.6B\n\n0.084\n\ncon\n\ntrol\n\nft\nrDra\n\nho\nAnc\n\nVoxtral-3B\n\nFigure 3: Runtime and training-correction measurements. (a) Runtime speed change for the 1.7B target on five evaluation sets. (b)\nChange in accepted length and cost-inclusive latency under the correct-position intervention. (c) Correct-position accepted-length\ngain and cost-inclusive pointer timing across 19 settings. (d) Remaining correct-position gain before and after AnchorDraft.\n\nevaluation set, while the maximum absolute WER change is\n0.01 percentage point. The five-set speed gain spans +6.4%–\n+7.1% across the two 0.6B seeds and +3.9%–+5.0% across\nthe three 1.7B seeds. The post-hoc 0.6B sensitivity sweep\nremains positive around the selected weight (table 18). The\nVoxtral control changes by −0.011 where position error remains stable during continuation.\nShared Mechanism. The overlap experiment distinguishes\nbetter position maintenance from a general increase in draft\nconfidence. We apply the same correct-position intervention\nbefore and after training. The remaining gain falls at both\nscales, and both reduction intervals exclude zero (fig. 3d). The\ngain remains positive after training. Alignment supervision\ntherefore removes part of the position-sensitive continuation\nerror without replacing the runtime correction.\nCombining the Corrections. The positive correct-position\ngain after training suggests that the runtime readout can\nstill recover useful work. We test this in a separate fiveset experiment at 1.7B. The control, AnchorDraft, and\nthe stacked system reach 1.472×, 1.531×, and 1.562× over\nautoregressive decoding, respectively. Adding runtime correction after training gives a further +1.8% gain with 95% CI\n[+0.9%, +2.7%], while the maximum absolute WER change\nremains 0.01 percentage points. The corrections remain useful together despite addressing some of the same continuation\nerror. Supp. table 20 gives absolute speed intervals.\nSystem Boundary. We next test whether a complete recognizer provides a better quality-cost tradeoff than the shallow\ndraft. In the same-slice comparison, the full recognizer adds\n600M parameters and 1.46 GiB of static weights. It accepts\nmore tokens but reaches only 0.59–0.70× autoregressive\n\nspeed. The shallow draft has 76.2M trainable parameters and\nreaches 1.44–1.61× on the same slices. Peak allocated memory remains target-dominated in these serial batch-one runs,\nso the matched comparison reports added static weight rather\nthan a peak-memory ratio. The full recognizer maintains\nposition at too much cost, while the shallow draft preserves\nthe quality-cost balance. A separate Whisper comparison\ngives the same audio-access ordering. Supp. Sections D.3,\nE.2 and F.2 and Section F give the training checks, seed-level\nresults, and system protocols.\n\n6\n\nConclusion and Limitations\n\nAlignment drift is one source of continuation failure in singlemodel speculative ASR. The restart and continuation split\nlocates the loss between target passes. Correct-position interventions recover part of the loss, while wrong-position\nwindows reduce acceptance. Verification attention supplies\na fresh position during decoding. AnchorDraft trains the\ndraft to maintain it without changing inference. Both improve\ncontinuation, provided that the recovered accepted length\ncovers the correction cost.\nThe position intervention explains only part of the rejected\nproposals. The study uses greedy verification that is lossless\nunder exact arithmetic, while low-precision computation\ncan change a tied argmax. AnchorDraft requires token-toframe supervision. Runtime correction is tested at two Qwen\nscales under low-batch, short-form decoding with a separate\nreadout. Other architectures and serving conditions require\nnew measurements. Extension to other continuous-memory\ntasks remains a hypothesis.\n\n\fReferences\nCai, T.; Li, Y.; Geng, Z.; Peng, H.; Lee, J. D.; Chen, D.; and\nDao, T. 2024. MEDUSA: Simple LLM inference acceleration\nframework with multiple decoding heads. In Proceedings\nof the 41st International Conference on Machine Learning,\nICML’24. JMLR.org.\nChan, W.; Jaitly, N.; Le, Q.; and Vinyals, O. 2016. Listen, attend and spell: A neural network for large vocabulary\nconversational speech recognition. In 2016 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing\n(ICASSP), 4960–4964.\nChen, C.; Borgeaud, S.; Irving, G.; Lespiau, J.-B.; Sifre, L.;\nand Jumper, J. 2023. Accelerating Large Language Model\nDecoding with Speculative Sampling. arXiv:2302.01318.\nChen, G.; Chai, S.; Wang, G.; Du, J.; Zhang, W.-Q.; Weng,\nC.; Su, D.; Povey, D.; Trmal, J.; Zhang, J.; Jin, M.; et al. 2021.\nGigaSpeech: An Evolving, Multi-domain ASR Corpus with\n10,000 Hours of Transcribed Audio. In Proc. Interspeech\n2021.\nCheng, Y.; Zhang, A.; Zhang, X.; Wang, C.; and Wang, Y.\n2024. Recurr